('ee9805e9-e129-43e9-882a-4eb559b4d3c2', 'grammar_punctuation', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence uses the apostrophe correctly?', '["The student’s projects were displayed in the hall, and all thirty were excellent.","The students projects’ were displayed in the hall.","The students’ projects were displayed in the hall.","The students project’s were displayed in the hall."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The projects belong to more than one student, so the apostrophe goes after the plural s.', 'AC9E6LY09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('f785a5ad-3464-4df3-8c95-c0b256c2dc2d', 'grammar_punctuation', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence says the same thing as "The council repaired the bridge", but in the passive voice?', '["The council was repairing the bridge.","The bridge repaired the council.","The council had repaired the bridge.","The bridge was repaired by the council."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the passive voice the thing being acted on comes first and the doer is introduced with "by". The other options change the meaning or only change the tense.', 'AC9E6LY09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('221bea14-10d0-4e2c-857a-708ce7d5b1ae', 'grammar_punctuation', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Choose the correct punctuation.
____ going to need more time to finish.', '["Their","They’re","There","Theyre"]'::jsonb, 1, '"They’re" is short for "they are", which is what the sentence needs. "Their" shows possession and "there" refers to a place.', 'AC9E6LY09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('72f9ac74-bcad-4429-b057-7bd03a039b3d', 'grammar_punctuation', 'grade_6', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?', '["The rain stopped; and the game continued.","The rain stopped; but we were soaked.","The rain stopped; the game continued.","The rain stopped; because the clouds cleared."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A semicolon joins two complete sentences that are closely related. It is not used before a conjunction such as "and", "but" or "because".', 'AC9E6LY09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('08a008b0-e12f-4810-8a28-5ccf8ac146ef', 'grammar_punctuation', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which word is the preposition in this sentence?
She left her bag under the desk.', '["under","left","her","desk"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A preposition shows the relationship between a noun and another part of the sentence, usually in place or time. "Under" tells us where the bag is.', 'AC9E6LY09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('cbed11dc-e0f6-4b51-9b09-66b9c607aeff', 'grammar_punctuation', 'grade_6', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence contains a subordinate clause?', '["Although the bell rang, nobody moved.","The bell rang and everybody left.","The bell rang loudly.","The bell rang, then it stopped."]'::jsonb, 0, '"Although the bell rang" cannot stand on its own as a sentence, which makes it a subordinate clause.', 'AC9E6LY09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('eb0b8cd8-ba9b-4612-9294-50da33176639', 'grammar_punctuation', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence uses quotation marks correctly?', '["\"Wait for me\" called Ravi.","\"Wait for me.\" called Ravi.","\"Wait for me,\" called Ravi.","Wait for me, \"called Ravi.\""]'::jsonb, 2, 'The comma goes inside the quotation marks, and the speech tag that follows begins with a lower-case letter.', 'AC9E6LY09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('17e73c13-bac0-4e0c-8ba7-15f80a28a888', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'One word in this sentence is spelt incorrectly. Write the correct spelling of that word.
The witness gave a consistant account of the event.', null, null, 'The correct spelling is "consistent".', 'AC9E8LY09', null, null, true, 'consistent', null, null, null),
('81d05ecb-9dc9-47a9-b88c-6c0f248f1850', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'One word in this sentence is spelt incorrectly. Write the correct spelling of that word.
He was determined to acheive a better result.', null, null, 'The correct spelling is "achieve".', 'AC9E8LY09', null, null, true, 'achieve', null, null, null),
('98cde5d7-7b95-468d-a696-bcd5544d60af', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'One word in this sentence is spelt incorrectly. Write the correct spelling of that word.
The two accounts were not compatable.', null, null, 'The correct spelling is "compatible".', 'AC9E8LY09', null, null, true, 'compatible', null, null, null),
('3052e460-e166-4059-8503-b715f5b9c1d4', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'One word in this sentence is spelt incorrectly. Write the correct spelling of that word.
She questioned the relevence of his argument.', null, null, 'The correct spelling is "relevance".', 'AC9E8LY09', null, null, true, 'relevance', null, null, null),
('3c80a4fd-9973-4edb-818d-b325c82166b5', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'One word in this sentence is spelt incorrectly. Write the correct spelling of that word.
The report contained a noticable error.', null, null, 'The correct spelling is "noticeable".', 'AC9E8LY09', null, null, true, 'noticeable', null, null, null),
('91c06eb8-e42c-45b0-ae21-5bc8e1951372', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence corrects the dangling modifier?
Walking through the forest, the birds were singing loudly.', '["Walking through the forest, the birds sang loudly.","The birds, walking through the forest, were singing loudly.","While walking through the forest, we heard the birds singing loudly.","Walking loudly through the forest, the birds were singing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'In the original, the opening phrase appears to describe the birds, implying the birds were walking. Naming who was walking fixes it.', 'AC9E8LY08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('85b282ca-0378-480c-9f60-730bcdcec9b3', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence uses the colon correctly?', '["She needed: a map, a torch and a compass.","She needed three things, a map: a torch and a compass.","She needed three things: and they were a map, a torch and a compass.","She needed three things: a map, a torch and a compass."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A colon follows a complete statement and introduces what comes next. "She needed:" is not a complete statement on its own.', 'AC9E8LY08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('37d40bb5-1bf1-4cff-8cb4-2d36702ccffb', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence is in the active voice?', '["Mistakes were made by the committee.","The committee made mistakes.","It was decided that mistakes had been made.","Mistakes had been made."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In the active voice the subject performs the action. The other options use the passive voice, which allows the doer to be hidden.', 'AC9E8LY08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('daf8d8af-3282-4ffe-b0f6-c439d457edf3', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence correctly uses a relative pronoun?', '["The author, which wrote the report, was unavailable.","The author whom wrote the report was unavailable.","The author who wrote the report was unavailable.","The author, that wrote the report was unavailable."]'::jsonb, 2, '"Who" is used for people and acts as the subject of "wrote". "Which" is used for things, and "whom" would only be correct as an object.', 'AC9E8LY08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('2d8dbb2d-8295-4b31-a65a-89a57b37a4ce', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence avoids the comma splice?', '["The results were clear; we published them immediately.","The results were clear, we published them immediately.","The results were clear, we published, them immediately.","The results were clear we published them immediately."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A comma splice joins two complete sentences with only a comma. A semicolon, a full stop, or a comma plus a conjunction all fix it.', 'AC9E8LY08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('4cb9c8ba-2ca3-45c7-8553-8fb9d612088d', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence uses the subjunctive correctly?', '["If I were in charge, I would change the timetable.","If I was in charge, I would change the timetable.","If I am in charge, I would change the timetable.","If I will be in charge, I would change the timetable."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The subjunctive "were" is used for a hypothetical situation that is not the case, which is why it pairs with "would".', 'AC9E8LY08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('bac1b949-4abb-486e-ae9b-f31834c60705', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence keeps a consistent tense?', '["She opened the door and sees the parcel on the step.","She opens the door and saw the parcel on the step.","She opened the door and saw the parcel on the step.","She opens the door and will see the parcel on the step."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both verbs are in the past tense. The other options shift between past and present within one sentence.', 'AC9E8LY08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('fae07aaf-c997-4aaf-b94d-d91c1768300d', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A rectangle measures √50 cm by √8 cm. Written in simplest surd form, its area is', '["10√2 cm²","√58 cm²","20 cm²","40 cm²"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Area = √50 × √8 = √400 = 20 cm². Alternatively 5√2 × 2√2 = 10 × 2 = 20.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('cc36aaa2-18eb-4606-88ef-45d951f113d6', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Simplify √75 − √27, giving your answer in simplest surd form.', '["2√3","√48","4√3","√102"]'::jsonb, 0, '√75 = 5√3 and √27 = 3√3, so the difference is 5√3 − 3√3 = 2√3.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('71d378db-8066-4711-bf9b-ff80de443ca2', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The mass of a grain of sand is about 0.000 004 5 kg. In scientific notation this is', '["4.5 × 10⁶ kg","45 × 10⁻⁷ kg","4.5 × 10⁻⁵ kg","4.5 × 10⁻⁶ kg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Moving the decimal point six places to the right gives 4.5, so the number is 4.5 × 10⁻⁶. Option C has the right value but is not in scientific notation, which needs a number between 1 and 10.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('1dafe337-318b-4a9d-ad95-ea62a7dc74a6', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Simplify (2x³y)⁴ ÷ (4x⁵y²).', '["8x⁷y²","4x⁷y²","4x¹²y⁴","2x⁷y²"]'::jsonb, 1, '(2x³y)⁴ = 16x¹²y⁴. Dividing by 4x⁵y² gives 16 ÷ 4 = 4, x¹² ÷ x⁵ = x⁷ and y⁴ ÷ y² = y², so the result is 4x⁷y².', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('a10e3bcd-cf80-47c2-b650-0632776ecfd0', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Evaluate 8^(−2/3).', '["−4","1/4","4","1/16"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The negative index means take the reciprocal, and the fractional index means the cube root squared: 8^(2/3) = (∛8)² = 2² = 4, so 8^(−2/3) = 1/4.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('82c5107d-c853-4e9d-a517-bf34e9400856', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A square has an area of 48 cm². Its side length, in simplest surd form, is', '["2√12 cm","24 cm","6√2 cm","4√3 cm"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The side is √48. Since 48 = 16 × 3, √48 = 4√3 cm. Option B is equal in value but not in simplest form.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('c5137f27-5a0c-42ef-80a1-1d5249c2e0de', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Rationalise the denominator of 6/√3.', '["2√3","6√3","√2","3√2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Multiplying top and bottom by √3 gives 6√3/3 = 2√3.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('41e7faa5-12af-4a09-97bf-8f5afb0ec797', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these is closest in value to √40?', '["5.7","20","6.3","6.9"]'::jsonb, 2, '6² = 36 and 7² = 49, so √40 lies between 6 and 7, much nearer 6. Checking, 6.3² = 39.69.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('75234c76-9386-4660-83ce-df43de87a635', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Factorise fully: 3x² − 12.', '["3(x² − 4)","(3x − 6)(x + 2)","3(x − 4)(x + 3)","3(x − 2)(x + 2)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Take out the common factor 3 first: 3(x² − 4). The bracket is a difference of two squares, so it factorises to 3(x − 2)(x + 2). Option B is correct but not fully factorised.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('bc83d938-2514-4adb-b8a6-a254a85dd4aa', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Solve x² + 2x − 15 = 0.', '["x = −3 or x = 5","x = 3 or x = −5","x = 3 or x = 5","x = 15 or x = −1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two numbers multiplying to −15 and adding to 2 are 5 and −3, so (x + 5)(x − 3) = 0, giving x = −5 or x = 3.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('48147dcf-4399-4d8c-9c0e-981f4b7336d6', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Expand and simplify (2x − 3)².', '["4x² + 9","4x² − 6x + 9","4x² − 12x + 9","2x² − 12x + 9"]'::jsonb, 2, '(2x − 3)² = (2x)² − 2 × 2x × 3 + 3² = 4x² − 12x + 9. Option B forgets the middle term.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('f798eaf0-8c31-422e-b0cd-c00aa664965c', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A rectangular garden is 3 m longer than it is wide, and its area is 40 m². If the width is w metres, the equation to solve is', '["w² + 3w − 40 = 0","w² + 3w + 40 = 0","2w + 3 = 40","w² − 3w − 40 = 0"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The length is w + 3, so the area is w(w + 3) = 40, which rearranges to w² + 3w − 40 = 0. Solving gives w = 5, so the garden is 5 m by 8 m.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('9f7deab6-167b-4f17-a61a-05337e7200de', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The line through (−1, 5) and (3, −3) has gradient', '["−2","2","−1/2","−8"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gradient = (change in y) ÷ (change in x) = (−3 − 5) ÷ (3 − (−1)) = −8 ÷ 4 = −2.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('65df085e-9719-44e8-b8b1-9a5da0ff05db', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Solve simultaneously: 3x + 2y = 16 and x − 2y = 0.', '["x = 2, y = 4","x = 4, y = 6","x = 4, y = 2","x = 6, y = 3"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Adding the two equations eliminates y: 4x = 16, so x = 4. Substituting into x − 2y = 0 gives y = 2. Checking in the first equation: 12 + 4 = 16.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('b8e5b1a6-5da6-4a37-84dc-10c54c85542a', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The parabola y = (x − 2)² − 9 has x-intercepts at', '["x = 2 and x = −9","x = −1 and x = 5","x = −5 and x = 1","x = 3 and x = −3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Setting y = 0 gives (x − 2)² = 9, so x − 2 = ±3 and x = 5 or x = −1.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('e20df3d9-2bad-4ebc-9e92-792e6d0f759d', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The turning point of y = (x + 4)² − 7 is', '["(4, −7)","(−4, 7)","(7, −4)","(−4, −7)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the form y = (x − h)² + k the turning point is (h, k). Here h = −4 and k = −7, so the turning point is (−4, −7).', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('cbe0df30-7aae-4c64-8300-9c1cf8e53bd3', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Make r the subject of the formula A = πr².', '["r = A/π","r = (A/π)²","r = √(A/π)","r = √A/π"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dividing both sides by π gives r² = A/π, then taking the positive square root gives r = √(A/π).', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('9938764c-9fdd-4600-ab44-a4800345901d', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which equation describes a quantity that starts at 200 and halves each year?', '["y = 200 × 0.5ˣ","y = 200 − 0.5x","y = 200 × 1.5ˣ","y = 0.5 × 200ˣ"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Halving repeatedly is exponential decay: the starting value is multiplied by 0.5 once per year, giving y = 200 × 0.5ˣ. Option B decreases by a fixed amount rather than a fixed proportion.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('9e290ad9-9bfe-4190-97c6-b52112b266a0', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'A right-angled triangle has legs of 6 cm and 8 cm. The length of the hypotenuse is', '["14 cm","√14 cm","48 cm","10 cm"]'::jsonb, 3, 'By Pythagoras, the hypotenuse squared is 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100, so the hypotenuse is 10 cm.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('42d3b920-d704-4b1f-be10-d7f601b169bc', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'An equilateral triangle has sides of 2 cm. Its exact height is', '["2√3 cm","√3 cm","1 cm","√2 cm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The height splits the triangle into two right-angled triangles with hypotenuse 2 and base 1, so the height is √(4 − 1) = √3 cm.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('ca19657d-e04a-4b48-86ef-abe16a5be7f2', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A cylinder has radius r and height h. If the radius is doubled and the height is unchanged, the volume is', '["twice as large","four times as large","eight times as large","unchanged"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume = πr²h. Doubling r squares to give 4r², so the volume is multiplied by 4. The height is unchanged and contributes no extra factor.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('b179a874-a2f2-4322-adb2-a55ec300a756', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Two similar triangles have corresponding sides in the ratio 2 : 5. The ratio of their areas is', '["2 : 5","8 : 125","10 : 25","4 : 25"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Area scales with the square of the length ratio, so the areas are in the ratio 2² : 5² = 4 : 25.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('70eb58e5-ddad-4b2c-be83-4fe846846afd', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The exact value of sin(60°) is', '["√3/2","1/2","√2/2","√3"]'::jsonb, 0, 'From the 30-60-90 triangle with sides 1, √3 and 2, sin(60°) = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = √3/2.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('5fca1100-2655-457c-ad58-4e83b0c67cc7', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A cone and a cylinder have the same radius and the same height. The volume of the cone is', '["half of the cylinder","equal to the cylinder","one third of the cylinder","three times the cylinder"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The cylinder has volume πr²h and the cone has volume (1/3)πr²h, so the cone is exactly one third.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('7a15ccc5-134a-4a2b-ab95-25480bdb2252', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A sector of a circle has an angle of 90° and radius 4 cm. Its exact area is', '["16π cm²","2π cm²","8π cm²","4π cm²"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A 90° sector is a quarter of the circle. The whole circle has area π × 4² = 16π, so the sector is 4π cm².', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('456f4200-20ca-492b-8d93-4d479e341b8b', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A bag holds 4 red and 6 green counters. Two are drawn without replacement. The probability that both are red is', '["4/25","2/15","8/45","2/5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first draw is red with probability 4/10. After removing one red, the second is red with probability 3/9. Multiplying gives (4/10) × (3/9) = 12/90 = 2/15.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('c16fb2d1-019b-4923-8498-958687613455', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'For mutually exclusive events A and B with Pr(A) = 0.35 and Pr(B) = 0.25, Pr(A or B) is', '["0.0875","0.1","0.6","0.9"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mutually exclusive events cannot both happen, so Pr(A or B) = Pr(A) + Pr(B) = 0.35 + 0.25 = 0.6. No overlap needs subtracting.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('c09f09ae-0de2-462f-802b-de1ae9ad9a12', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'A data set has a median of 24 and an interquartile range of 10, with Q1 = 19. The value of Q3 is', '["29","34","14","24"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The interquartile range is Q3 − Q1, so Q3 = 19 + 10 = 29.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('55fcaa6d-656a-470f-95ac-9cf5e2929f80', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Adding a value far above every other value to a data set will', '["increase the mean much more than the median","increase the median much more than the mean","leave both unchanged","decrease the mean"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mean uses every value, so one extreme value pulls it noticeably. The median depends only on position, so it shifts at most one place along the ordered list.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('391416ae-6e0f-4a5f-a2f9-26eef2a3943a', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sample is most likely to be representative of all students at a large school?', '["The first thirty students to arrive one morning","All students in the school band","Every twentieth name on the full school roll","Students who volunteer to take part"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Taking every twentieth name from the full roll gives every student the same chance of selection. The other three are biased towards early arrivers, musicians, or the kind of student who volunteers.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('7d28cedc-32ba-4fab-aa34-eba7a76b374d', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A $12 000 car depreciates by 18% of its value each year. Its value after 3 years, to the nearest dollar, is', '["$5520","$6720","$6612","$8071"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each year the car keeps 82% of its value, so after 3 years it is worth 12 000 × 0.82³ = $6612 to the nearest dollar. Option B subtracts a flat 18% of the original price each year instead.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('2c8901de-b7cb-409d-9dc7-cea37d37d1b6', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', '$4500 is invested at 5.4% per annum compounding monthly. Its value after 4 years, to the nearest dollar, is', '["$5584","$5472","$5540","$5715"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The monthly rate is 5.4 ÷ 12 = 0.45%, and 4 years is 48 months, so the value is 4500 × 1.0045⁴⁸ = $5584. Option B uses simple interest.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('5e685612-ef1c-4ef0-89d9-6b5640ef128f', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A shop marks up its stock by 40%, then holds a sale taking 40% off the marked price. Compared with the original cost, the sale price is', '["the same","16% higher","20% lower","16% lower"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two changes multiply rather than cancel: 1.40 × 0.60 = 0.84, which is 84% of the original, a fall of 16%.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('b68249df-2413-4f50-9178-21dbe4fbd93b', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The distance to a star is 4.1 × 10¹³ km. A spacecraft travels at 6.0 × 10⁴ km/h. The journey time, in hours, is closest to', '["2.5 × 10¹⁸","6.8 × 10⁸","6.8 × 10⁹","1.5 × 10⁻⁹"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Time = distance ÷ speed = (4.1 × 10¹³) ÷ (6.0 × 10⁴) = 6.83 × 10⁸ hours. Option B multiplies instead of dividing.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('290cc990-afa0-413a-bcdd-f7d6640b5e0c', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A population of 8000 grows by 3.5% per year. The number of whole years before it first exceeds 12 000 is', '["11","12","14","10"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Solving 8000 × 1.035ⁿ > 12 000 gives 1.035ⁿ > 1.5, so n > 11.8. The first whole year at which it exceeds 12 000 is year 12.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('feba004b-ded1-46b5-9697-277d8e00e57c', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'A recipe for 6 people needs 750 g of flour. For 10 people the amount needed is', '["1150 g","1500 g","1050 g","1250 g"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The amount per person is 750 ÷ 6 = 125 g, so 10 people need 1250 g.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('d328ceea-f0bc-4a5f-9973-8aa54f1b1b4b', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A map has a scale of 1 : 25 000. Two towns are 8.4 cm apart on the map. The real distance is', '["2.1 km","21 km","210 km","0.21 km"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The real distance is 8.4 × 25 000 = 210 000 cm. Dividing by 100 000 gives 2.1 km.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('ea37de0b-91fa-4b62-a4ea-92767566fbad', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A quantity increases from 240 to 306. The percentage increase is', '["21.6%","66%","27.5%","78.4%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The increase is 306 − 240 = 66. As a percentage of the original, 66 ÷ 240 = 0.275, so 27.5%. Option B divides by the new value instead of the original.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('f9d480cf-c4b6-411e-84e8-835cb7eb4504', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Using the quadratic formula, the solutions of 2x² − 5x − 4 = 0, correct to two decimal places, are', '["x = 2.50 or x = −0.80","x = 3.14 or x = 0.64","x = 1.25 or x = −1.60","x = 3.14 or x = −0.64"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With a = 2, b = −5 and c = −4, the discriminant is 25 + 32 = 57, so x = (5 ± √57) ÷ 4, giving 3.14 and −0.64.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('7d67b4ca-735d-4bf6-ae52-499f6c822d4f', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ball is thrown so that its height in metres after t seconds is h = 20t − 5t². The greatest height it reaches is', '["15 m","20 m","25 m","40 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The parabola has zeros at t = 0 and t = 4, so the maximum is halfway between, at t = 2. Then h = 40 − 20 = 20 m.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('24000870-6ac5-4b0d-a153-69cc7004cd34', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'For the same ball, h = 20t − 5t², the time at which it returns to the ground is', '["2 seconds","5 seconds","4 seconds","20 seconds"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Setting h = 0 gives 5t(4 − t) = 0, so t = 0 at launch and t = 4 on landing.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('1b2644a4-5063-434e-a82f-be4aeaca94cd', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A phone plan charges a $15 monthly fee plus 12 cents per minute. The number of minutes that makes the monthly bill $45 is', '["250","375","200","300"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The call cost is 45 − 15 = $30. At $0.12 per minute that is 30 ÷ 0.12 = 250 minutes.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('0f3a7cea-d5d9-4fa7-8864-25cf60a80440', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Two hire companies charge as follows: A charges $60 plus $0.40 per km, B charges $30 plus $0.70 per km. The distance at which they cost the same is', '["100 km","75 km","120 km","90 km"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Setting 60 + 0.4d = 30 + 0.7d gives 30 = 0.3d, so d = 100 km. Below 100 km B is cheaper; above it, A is.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('4c7901c9-53ea-4dd0-abc1-f7804b8f60db', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The value of x for which 3ˣ = 200, correct to two decimal places, is', '["5.30","4.19","4.82","66.67"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Taking logarithms of both sides gives x = log(200) ÷ log(3) = 4.82. Checking, 3⁴·⁸² is approximately 200.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('881428f9-0926-4db2-b0e0-62f5748d2584', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In triangle ABC, angle A = 40°, angle B = 75° and side a = 12 cm. Using the sine rule, side b is closest to', '["12.4 cm","18.0 cm","15.6 cm","20.2 cm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sine rule gives b = a × sin(B) ÷ sin(A) = 12 × sin(75°) ÷ sin(40°) = 12 × 0.9659 ÷ 0.6428 = 18.0 cm.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('c2e91094-36f8-4156-ba3d-146bb1b5ba82', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A triangle has sides of 7 cm and 9 cm with an included angle of 62°. Its area is closest to', '["31.5 cm²","63.0 cm²","14.8 cm²","27.8 cm²"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Area = ½ab sin(C) = 0.5 × 7 × 9 × sin(62°) = 31.5 × 0.8829 = 27.8 cm². Option B forgets the sine factor.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('08005e58-e02c-4ff2-851a-64160643c47e', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A ladder 6 m long leans against a wall at 68° to the ground. The height it reaches up the wall is closest to', '["2.2 m","6.5 m","5.6 m","14.9 m"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The height is the opposite side: 6 × sin(68°) = 6 × 0.9272 = 5.6 m.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('40012aea-951b-43cf-a4ea-71f35f66c638', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A cylindrical tank has radius 1.2 m and height 2.5 m. Its capacity, in litres, is closest to', '["11 300 L","11.3 L","9400 L","18 850 L"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Volume = π × 1.2² × 2.5 = 11.31 m³. One cubic metre is 1000 litres, so the capacity is about 11 300 L.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('64649b31-7078-4b75-869e-33ea747cf71a', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A sphere has a radius of 6 cm. Its surface area is closest to', '["905 cm²","113 cm²","151 cm²","452 cm²"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Surface area = 4πr² = 4 × π × 36 = 452 cm². Option B doubles it; option C uses πr² only.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('57646da9-a703-4d82-8381-187580229d3f', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A rectangular box measures 3 m by 4 m by 12 m. The longest straight rod that fits inside is closest to', '["12.0 m","13.0 m","19.0 m","5.0 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The space diagonal is √(3² + 4² + 12²) = √(9 + 16 + 144) = √169 = 13.0 m.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('643079c0-5c4c-4292-b926-2a12e8f1b55e', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The box plots below compare the times, in minutes, taken by two classes to finish a task. Which statement is correct?', '["Class A has the higher median","Class B has the larger interquartile range","Class A has the larger range","The two classes have the same median"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Class A runs from Q1 = 14 to Q3 = 22, an interquartile range of 8. Class B runs from 12 to 26, an interquartile range of 14. Class B has the higher median, 21 against 18, and the ranges are 20 and 24.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('66171167-5d8b-42a4-9093-61dd4ff5dbce', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The table below shows the results of a survey. What percentage of those who play sport also play an instrument, to one decimal place?', '["27.5%","61.4%","22.0%","38.6%"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Of the 88 people who play sport, 34 also play an instrument. That is 34 ÷ 88 = 0.386, which is 38.6%. Option B divides by the whole survey of 124 instead.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('5280ccfb-0f9d-4323-a313-2bbc8d6d5af9', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Heights are normally distributed with a mean of 168 cm and a standard deviation of 7 cm. The percentage of people taller than 182 cm is closest to', '["2.5%","5%","16%","32%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The height 182 cm is two standard deviations above the mean, since 168 + 2 × 7 = 182. By the 68-95-99.7% rule, 5% lie outside two standard deviations, split evenly between the tails, so 2.5% are above.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('caa24c7a-49fe-4745-93dc-61afac2eff42', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A scatterplot of study hours against test score shows points close to a straight line sloping upwards. The correlation coefficient is most likely', '["−0.92","0.15","0.92","1.80"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Points close to an upward-sloping line indicate a strong positive association, so r is close to 1. Option B is negative, C is weak, and D is impossible because r always lies between −1 and 1.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('0af92a03-0ee7-4a9f-8971-5dc55ae4a95f', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinner has 5 equal sectors numbered 1 to 5. It is spun twice. The probability that the total is 8 or more is', '["3/25","1/5","8/25","6/25"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 25 equally likely outcomes include six totalling 8 or more: (3,5), (4,4), (4,5), (5,3), (5,4) and (5,5). The probability is therefore 6/25.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('3f53231a-1421-4160-846f-2cef9e8aec96', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In a bag of 20 marbles, 8 are blue. Two marbles are drawn without replacement. The probability that neither is blue, to three decimal places, is', '["0.360","0.347","0.160","0.421"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first is not blue with probability 12/20, and then the second with probability 11/19. Multiplying gives 132/380 = 0.347.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('8902ffcf-2778-4ae0-8652-eae8ce244057', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A cinema sells adult tickets at $19.50 and child tickets at $13.50. One evening it sold 240 tickets and took $3894. The number of adult tickets sold was', '["131","120","109","98"]'::jsonb, 2, 'If a adult tickets were sold then 240 − a were child tickets, so 19.5a + 13.5(240 − a) = 3894. That gives 6a + 3240 = 3894, so a = 109. Checking: 109 × 19.5 + 131 × 13.5 = 2125.50 + 1768.50 = 3894.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('94f38538-5b51-4dcf-b5e1-c9b4154136f7', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A culture of bacteria is modelled by N = 500 × 1.8ᵗ, where t is in hours. The time taken to reach 10 000 bacteria, to one decimal place, is', '["5.1 hours","4.3 hours","6.2 hours","11.1 hours"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing gives 1.8ᵗ = 20, so t = log(20) ÷ log(1.8) = 5.1 hours. Option D divides 20 by 1.8 instead of using logarithms.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('676b11bb-0e4c-42e5-a7f7-94603f407b79', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A triangular garden bed has two sides of 8 m and 11 m with an included angle of 47°. The third side is closest to', '["8.1 m","13.0 m","9.6 m","6.4 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'By the cosine rule, c² = 8² + 11² − 2 × 8 × 11 × cos(47°) = 185 − 120.03 = 64.97, so c = 8.1 m. Option B adds the term instead of subtracting it.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('7b2ac16e-c02a-4ace-bda1-2f137fc613a3', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The frequency table below shows the scores of 25 students. The mean score is', '["3.00","2.50","2.84","3.55"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The total of all scores is 1 × 3 + 2 × 7 + 3 × 8 + 4 × 5 + 5 × 2 = 3 + 14 + 24 + 20 + 10 = 71. Dividing by the 25 students gives a mean of 2.84. Option B is the mode rather than the mean.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('71fdc765-bc3c-48e8-81d3-8c15448aa4ae', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does the grandfather reject the word "looking"?', '["He thought the work was too difficult to describe","He did not want to discuss his job","Looking implies knowing what you are after, and the dredge took whatever was there","He was not permitted to search for specific items"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The narrator explains the distinction directly: "Looking implied you knew what you were after", whereas the dredge simply raised whatever the riverbed happened to hold.', 'AC9E10LY05', '16cde21e-5438-40f6-a0d3-24b36a365731', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('dbc91c5d-32fc-4709-874b-c87f11f5c8de', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The list "Bicycle frames. A cash register. Four hundred and some shopping trolleys." is written as fragments rather than a full sentence. This has the effect of', '["conveying the sheer accumulated randomness of what came up","making the objects seem carefully catalogued and valued","suggesting the writer has forgotten most of the details","implying the objects were dangerous"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clipped, unconnected fragments imitate objects arriving one after another with no relationship to each other, which is the point being made about the riverbed.', 'AC9E10LY05', '16cde21e-5438-40f6-a0d3-24b36a365731', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('93883f8d-4eb3-40a2-a4af-cbdcda991601', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why is the detail that the Roman coin "turned out not to be Roman" included?', '["To show the grandfather was often mistaken","To suggest the river was older than people thought","To explain why the dredge was eventually replaced","To undercut the one glamorous item in the list and keep the tone unromantic"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The coin is the only find with any romance to it, and the clause immediately withdraws that romance — which keeps the passage from turning the work into an adventure.', 'AC9E10LY05', '16cde21e-5438-40f6-a0d3-24b36a365731', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('78e45e5d-f4ac-460f-aab2-a6fde86993b5', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The single-sentence paragraph "And, twice in thirty-one years, a person." is set apart in order to', '["show how rarely the dredge was used","give the fact the weight the list denied it","suggest the narrator is unsure of the number","indicate a change of narrator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Everything before it is a run of objects in a single paragraph. Isolating this line refuses to let a human being sit in that list, and the pause forces the reader to register it.', 'AC9E10LY05', '16cde21e-5438-40f6-a0d3-24b36a365731', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('33e3e29f-950a-499a-ae22-2d2f2eabe1d2', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What does the narrator realise partway through the grandfather’s account?', '["That the story was invented","That the story was really about the wallet, not the body","That his grandfather had been frightened by the work","That his grandfather wanted him to take up the same work"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The narrator says he "understood partway through that he was not telling me a story about a body. He was telling me a story about a wallet."', 'AC9E10LY05', '16cde21e-5438-40f6-a0d3-24b36a365731', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('0df36e85-ccc3-4d76-b1f2-9bb2f6c2cc88', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '"What he wanted me to take from this was... something about proportion." What does proportion mean here?', '["That the river was larger than people realised","That the grandfather had worked longer than most people","That photographs last longer than other materials","That thirty years of heavy objects mattered less than one small surviving thing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The paragraph sets three decades of hauled-up weight against four inches of card, and it is the card he could still describe forty years later.', 'AC9E10LY05', '16cde21e-5438-40f6-a0d3-24b36a365731', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('77e5cce6-6273-4173-a177-04c5dee71b0e', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the effect of the final paragraph’s description of the suction system?', '["It suggests that efficiency has removed the act of noticing","It celebrates a safer and more efficient method","It explains why the grandfather retired","It implies the river is now cleaner"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The new system raises everything the old one did, but "nothing is looked at on the way past" — so what is lost is not material but attention, and with it the stories that attention produced.', 'AC9E10LY05', '16cde21e-5438-40f6-a0d3-24b36a365731', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('f8548fbf-434c-4520-ab57-cc903b9610cf', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which best describes the narrator’s tone throughout?', '["Bitter and accusing","Nostalgic and sentimental","Restrained and reflective","Detached and uninterested"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The narrator withholds emotion — even the body is reported plainly — and works instead by turning the memory over. He resists sentiment while clearly finding the memory significant.', 'AC9E10LY05', '16cde21e-5438-40f6-a0d3-24b36a365731', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('8ab2126c-b4c6-4e7a-8822-19495c254daa', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'According to the writer, what does conceding a point early achieve?', '["It shows genuine open-mindedness","It confuses the opposing speaker","It shortens the argument","It costs the speaker nothing and buys a great deal of credibility"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The writer says the concession "costs them nothing and purchases a great deal", presenting it as a tactic rather than as honesty.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'f63ccae3-ee74-4da4-b6bc-980783818dc3', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('bbc7d9c8-830b-48b3-a962-a0ea2ed38224', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does the writer describe the passive voice as useful to this kind of speaker?', '["It sounds more formal and educated","It removes the person who did the thing","It makes sentences shorter","It is harder to understand"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The examples given — "mistakes were made, concerns have been raised" — report events with no actor, "so that no one is ever the person who did it".', 'AC9E10LY05', 'f63ccae3-ee74-4da4-b6bc-980783818dc3', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('8ee94c7f-fff9-4581-9ced-e61779e8e3a8', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does the writer call the pragmatic-versus-emotional framing "a neat trick"?', '["Because both words mean the same thing","Because pragmatic speakers are usually correct","Because objecting to the label appears to confirm it","Because it ends the discussion immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The writer explains that "the more strongly you object to being called emotional, the more emotional you appear" — the accusation is constructed so that resisting it counts as evidence for it.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'f63ccae3-ee74-4da4-b6bc-980783818dc3', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('ed5b6c4c-3f25-4f65-bca4-983baaf5f9ae', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '"None of this is lying. That is what makes it worth noticing." Why does the writer emphasise this?', '["Because a lie would be easier to identify and answer","To defend the speakers being described","To show the writer is being fair-minded","Because lying is a separate topic"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The danger the writer is describing lies precisely in a performance built from true sentences: there is nothing false to point at, yet the conclusion is still unsupported.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'f63ccae3-ee74-4da4-b6bc-980783818dc3', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('deefbc0c-34ec-4387-9097-6dc360836da3', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What point does the example of the tenant and the company lawyer make?', '["That composure is easiest for whoever has least at stake","That lawyers are better trained at arguing","That tenants are usually in the right","That legal disputes are decided unfairly"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The writer uses the pair to show the two speakers "are not equally free to be measured", so calmness reflects position rather than the strength of the case.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'f63ccae3-ee74-4da4-b6bc-980783818dc3', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('999a7ee4-fffe-4316-9228-c738824eeb01', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What concession does the writer make?', '["That the calm speaker is usually right after all","That tone should always be ignored","That anger is not an argument either","That the test proposed is unreliable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The writer states "I am not arguing for shouting. Anger is not an argument either", which narrows the claim and makes it harder to dismiss as a defence of outbursts.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'f63ccae3-ee74-4da4-b6bc-980783818dc3', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('8137ac09-9f88-45cc-9bbc-0fcc73dadd40', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What does the writer’s proposed test involve?', '["Recording the speakers and comparing their volume","Rewriting both speakers’ claims flatly and comparing them","Asking a neutral third party to judge","Reading the arguments rather than hearing them"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The final paragraph asks the reader to strip each speaker’s claims of concessions and passive constructions and set the flattened versions side by side.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'f63ccae3-ee74-4da4-b6bc-980783818dc3', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('2edbb113-79d9-4dae-b535-52d12c928ca8', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the writer’s central argument?', '["That calm speakers are dishonest","That emotional arguments are more truthful","That public debate should be conducted in writing","That we credit manner as though it were reasoning, and manner favours the powerful"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The writer states the claim as "narrower" than a condemnation: we "routinely credit a manner as though it were a reason, and the manner is cheapest for whoever has the most power in the room."', 'AC9E10LY05', 'f63ccae3-ee74-4da4-b6bc-980783818dc3', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('7b81e852-a395-48f5-b2a3-554616655697', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why had asking about the boat "become the wrong thing to do"?', '["Ada’s father had forbidden any mention of it","The boat was dangerous to climb on","The subject had become painful without ever being discussed","Her mother had sold it without telling anyone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing is ever stated, but the wordless tarpaulin replacements, the mother’s displaced complaint and the father’s silence all indicate a subject the family avoids rather than one that was banned.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'ca80ee66-fbcb-4c54-a54f-df4c7c7637bf', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('80e7e965-96d2-480b-9610-0454c8f724e9', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What does the detail that her father replaced the tarpaulin "wordlessly, every second autumn" suggest?', '["He has neither finished with the boat nor let it go","He intended to sell the boat","He resented having to maintain it","He had forgotten why the boat was there"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The upkeep is regular and deliberate, so he has not abandoned it; but it is silent and changes nothing, so he has not resumed it either.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'ca80ee66-fbcb-4c54-a54f-df4c7c7637bf', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('fe0ad41f-6276-4f28-a074-7e72e36d46b3', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The mother’s complaint about the 1994 trip is described as standing "in for something else". This suggests', '["she had actually enjoyed the trip","she had been unwell that day","the trip never took place","the real grievance is about the boat’s hold on the family, not the sailing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A complaint repeated "often" about a single outing thirty years ago is disproportionate to the outing, which is why the narrator reads it as a substitute for an unspoken objection.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'ca80ee66-fbcb-4c54-a54f-df4c7c7637bf', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('68763304-e17f-4db8-b6e6-60c731a479aa', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Why is the smell of diesel surprising to Ada?', '["Diesel is not used in boats of that kind","Nothing had run in the boat for twenty years","The boat had been cleaned recently","She had expected the cabin to smell of the sea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The text gives the reason directly: the smell surprised her "because nothing had run in it for twenty years".', 'AC9E10LY05', 'ca80ee66-fbcb-4c54-a54f-df4c7c7637bf', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('980af171-60c1-46ac-a741-7c46281378c5', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the significance of the pencil still in the groove of the chart table?', '["It proves someone had been aboard recently","It shows the boat was left mid-preparation, as though work would resume","It suggests the boat was used for teaching","It indicates the chart table was never finished"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A pencil left in place is the posture of an interrupted task rather than a finished one, which is what makes the abandonment poignant.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'ca80ee66-fbcb-4c54-a54f-df4c7c7637bf', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('62763ec7-cf44-48ec-bb18-d28dc37bae6e', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does the writer specify that the list was "not a short list. It was not a long one either"?', '["To show Ada could not count them accurately","To indicate the list was incomplete","To show the father was disorganised","To suggest the work was achievable, which makes stopping harder to explain"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Twenty-two items with eleven done is an ordinary, finishable job. The refusal to call it long removes the easy explanation that the task was simply too big.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'ca80ee66-fbcb-4c54-a54f-df4c7c7637bf', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('02140076-1c6a-4681-ba70-ae8955ffb800', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does Ada say nothing when she goes back inside?', '["She recognises the silence as something the family maintains together","She is afraid of being punished","She has not understood what she found","She intends to finish the boat as a surprise"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Her silence is matched by his — "her father did not ask where she had been" — and they sit through a programme neither is watching, which shows the avoidance is mutual rather than one-sided.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'ca80ee66-fbcb-4c54-a54f-df4c7c7637bf', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('8058371f-02ba-4188-b138-a10a201fc739', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why would Ada "rather not know which item it was"?', '["The list has since been lost","She no longer cares about the boat","Knowing the exact point he gave up would make the loss too specific","She believes he may still finish it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While the stopping point is unknown it stays an abstraction. Naming the item would fix the moment her father stopped believing he would sail, and that is what she chooses not to hold.', 'AC9E10LY05', 'ca80ee66-fbcb-4c54-a54f-df4c7c7637bf', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('c3f44bc8-36e1-46f5-8944-f86d673d9dd3', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does the writer mention asking "eleven times"?', '["To criticise the building’s staff","To explain why the article took so long to write","To suggest the room contains something secret","To show how difficult access was, and so how rarely the room is seen"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The detail follows immediately from "It is not open to the public", and the specific number conveys persistence against a closed door without the writer having to claim it.', 'AC9E10LY05', '793e2979-0458-4c5e-8b46-33a7b415d124', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('a0e513de-bf35-41a1-93e1-43b6fcd17edf', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What effect does arranging the items by year rather than by type produce?', '["It makes individual objects easier to locate","Each shelf shows everything a single year failed to reclaim","It keeps valuable items separate from worthless ones","It allows the room to hold more objects"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The writer explains the "strange effect" directly: a shelf holds not a category but a cross-section of one twelve-month period.', 'AC9E10LY05', '793e2979-0458-4c5e-8b46-33a7b415d124', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('dbb8e3b1-0e34-494a-a685-804c1cb28cf7', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does the writer call "curator" the "only accurate" title?', '["She was formally trained as a curator","The council refuses to give her a proper title","What she does — keeping, arranging and knowing a collection — is curatorship in everything but name","She curates a different museum as well"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The aside distinguishes the official label from the reality: she has kept and arranged a collection for nineteen years, which is the work whether or not the job description says so.', 'AC9E10LY05', '793e2979-0458-4c5e-8b46-33a7b415d124', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('b0ac0236-2eb9-4b3a-98ba-44eaba9da403', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the curator’s reason for saying the room is not sad?', '["Almost everything in it was replaced, so it records survivable losses","Most of the items were of little value","The owners will eventually return for them","She has grown used to the collection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'She argues that the umbrella, the phone and even the ring were each followed by the owner going on, so the room holds "four thousand things that turned out to be survivable" rather than four thousand losses.', 'AC9E10LY05', '793e2979-0458-4c5e-8b46-33a7b415d124', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('8762436f-e83f-47e2-b36a-e02f72377b35', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why does the writer not elaborate on the shelf of "unreplaceable" items?', '["He did not ask, and is unsure whether that was tact or a failure","The curator refused to discuss it","The items were too valuable to describe","He was not permitted to approach that shelf"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The writer states plainly that "I did not ask", then leaves the judgement open between "the most tactful thing I did that year or the worst piece of journalism".', 'AC9E10LY05', '793e2979-0458-4c5e-8b46-33a7b415d124', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('7e8dcfd8-1ccf-4f8b-94de-0df0acd18139', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the effect of leaving the unreplaceable shelf undescribed?', '["It suggests the writer is concealing evidence","It weakens the article by omitting its best material","It preserves the weight of those objects by refusing to turn them into anecdotes","It implies the curator was being untruthful"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every other object in the piece has been listed and slightly flattened by being listed. Withholding these keeps them from the same treatment, which is why the writer can call the silence tactful.', 'AC9E10LY05', '793e2979-0458-4c5e-8b46-33a7b415d124', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('658c2267-ad41-4ec1-8bcd-5906c35ddecf', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '"Nobody made that decision." What is the writer’s point here?', '["The council acted without proper authority","The room was created by nineteen years of nobody choosing to discard anything","The curator exceeded her responsibilities","The decision was made but never recorded"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The final sentence explains it: "the accumulated result of not deciding is a museum" — no one chose to found it, and it exists anyway.', 'AC9E10LY05', '793e2979-0458-4c5e-8b46-33a7b415d124', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('64400571-5155-4b8c-aa98-30e4940cd3fd', 'reading_literary_analysis', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which best describes the structure of this piece?', '["A chronological account of one day’s visit","An argument supported by statistics","A comparison between two collections","A description that opens out into a reflection on how the room came to be"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The writer moves from the room’s contents, to the curator’s view of them, to a closing observation about the absence of any decision — widening from object to meaning.', 'AC9E10LY05', '793e2979-0458-4c5e-8b46-33a7b415d124', null, true, null, null, null, null),
('dda5644b-72c3-4c82-b645-8974a4158632', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'One word in this sentence is spelt incorrectly. Write the correct spelling of that word.
