('b15f1744-f54f-4dcf-9b09-946220aece89', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence correctly uses the semicolon?', '["The report was late; because the data arrived on Friday.","The report was late; and the data arrived on Friday.","The report was late; the data had arrived only on Friday.","The report; was late because of the data."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A semicolon joins two statements that could each stand alone. It is not used before a conjunction such as "because" or "and", and never between a subject and its verb.', 'AC9E10LA08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('8e928b35-0a11-4286-9a46-7556e42f5499', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence avoids the misplaced modifier?
Having reviewed the evidence, the conclusion was obvious.', '["Having reviewed the evidence, the panel found the conclusion obvious.","Having reviewed the evidence, the conclusion was obvious to the panel.","The conclusion, having reviewed the evidence, was obvious.","The conclusion was obvious, having reviewed the evidence."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The opening phrase must describe the subject that follows it. Only the panel can review evidence; a conclusion cannot, so the panel has to be the subject.', 'AC9E10LA08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('f529df29-60c0-4f53-a559-781f15d6b4bc', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence uses "fewer" and "less" correctly?', '["There were less students and fewer noise this year.","There were fewer students and fewer noise this year.","There were less students and less noise this year.","There were fewer students and less noise this year."]'::jsonb, 3, '"Fewer" is used for things that can be counted individually, such as students; "less" is used for quantities that cannot, such as noise.', 'AC9E10LA08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('a55c7062-dfc4-4052-a59c-c8ffa0297193', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["The novel which won the prize, has been translated into nine languages.","The novel, which won the prize, has been translated into nine languages.","The novel, which won the prize has been translated into nine languages.","The novel which, won the prize, has been translated into nine languages."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clause "which won the prize" adds non-essential information, so it takes a comma at both ends. Opening the pair without closing it, or the reverse, is an error.', 'AC9E10LA08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('89c0e01a-c635-4010-81d9-c2c3b8f810a5', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence maintains parallel structure?', '["The course covers writing clearly, editing carefully and how to research.","The course covers writing clearly, editing carefully and researching thoroughly.","The course covers clear writing, careful editing and to research.","The course covers writing clearly, careful editing and research methods."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Items in a list should share a grammatical form. Only this option uses three matching "-ing" phrases; the others mix participles, nouns and infinitives.', 'AC9E10LA08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('c86b203c-792c-4115-8442-ebf6e3310cf2', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence uses the apostrophe correctly?', '["The witness’s accounts differed from one another.","The witnesses accounts’ differed from one another.","The witnesses account’s differed from one another.","The witnesses’ accounts differed from one another."]'::jsonb, 3, 'More than one witness gave an account, so the apostrophe follows the plural "witnesses". Option B would mean a single witness.', 'AC9E10LA08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('5f311cee-4b00-4131-8f74-ddde671ecba9', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence correctly uses "who" rather than "whom"?', '["The candidate who was selected by the panel withdrew.","The candidate who the panel selected withdrew.","The candidate whom was selected withdrew.","The candidate, who the panel selected him, withdrew."]'::jsonb, 0, '"Who" is correct when it is the subject of the verb that follows — here "who was selected". "Whom" would be needed only where it is the object.', 'AC9E10LA08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('20f162dc-04d1-4621-9b09-ea2f263dfe21', 'grammar_punctuation', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which sentence corrects the shift in tense?
She entered the room, looks around, and sat down.', '["She enters the room, looks around, and sat down.","She entered the room, looks around, and sits down.","She entered the room, looked around, and sat down.","She enters the room, looked around, and sits down."]'::jsonb, 2, 'All three verbs should be in the same tense. Only this option keeps every verb in the past.', 'AC9E10LA08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('fe6b303e-2c4d-4d73-83ac-a054ca292bc8', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The graph below shows y = x² − 4x + 3. Write down the x-coordinate of its turning point.', null, null, 'The parabola is symmetric about its turning point, and its x-intercepts are 1 and 3. The turning point sits halfway between them, at x = 2.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, '2', ARRAY['x = 2']::text[], null, null),
('68d2708f-3704-4bdb-a3c4-374315ae8abc', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'For the graph below, write down the y-intercept of y = x² − 4x + 3.', null, null, 'The y-intercept is the value when x = 0, which is 0 − 0 + 3 = 3.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, '3', ARRAY['y = 3', '(0, 3)']::text[], null, null),
('0e1b7774-1c8f-4c84-8408-ab505658cb9a', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The graph below shows a parabola. Its rule is', '["y = (x − 1)² + 4","y = −(x + 1)² + 4","y = −(x − 1)² + 4","y = (x + 1)² − 4"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The curve opens downwards, so the coefficient is negative. Its turning point is at (1, 4), which gives y = −(x − 1)² + 4.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('9e44c4ea-d7bb-4132-8470-1f8802b48666', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The graph below shows y = 2x − 3. Write down its gradient.', null, null, 'The line rises 2 units for every 1 unit across, so the gradient is 2. It is also the coefficient of x in y = 2x − 3.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, '2', ARRAY['m = 2']::text[], null, null),
('7fcd2176-3023-4da8-9857-67f3b69b9c40', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The two lines below intersect at', '["(3, 5)","(5, 3)","(2, 4)","(4, 6)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Setting x + 2 = −2x + 11 gives 3x = 9, so x = 3 and y = 5. Reading the graph, the lines cross at (3, 5).', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('62fa7fad-7297-4d51-a4a2-898f82483059', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The graph below shows the height of a ball, in metres, t seconds after it is thrown. Write down the greatest height it reaches, in metres.', null, null, 'The curve peaks halfway between its two zeros at t = 0 and t = 4, so at t = 2, where the height is 20 × 2 − 5 × 4 = 20 metres.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, '20', ARRAY['20 m', '20 metres']::text[], null, null),
('4b1f06d3-ae02-4b12-9ad4-a3a1da0345ac', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'From the graph below, write down the number of seconds the ball is in the air.', null, null, 'The ball is in the air until the curve returns to height zero, which the graph shows at t = 4 seconds.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, '4', ARRAY['4 s', '4 seconds']::text[], null, null),
('aa0def98-588c-4790-b183-4d5efb457840', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The graph below is of y = 2ˣ. As x decreases, the curve', '["crosses the x-axis at x = 0","becomes negative","levels out at y = 1","approaches zero without ever reaching it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An exponential curve has the x-axis as a horizontal asymptote: 2ˣ is always positive, so the graph gets ever closer to zero without touching it.', 'AC9M10A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('36641b11-07ec-4e16-a2bf-1acaf434fe7d', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The right-angled triangle below has the measurements shown. Write down the length of the hypotenuse, in centimetres.', null, null, 'By Pythagoras, the hypotenuse squared is 12² + 5² = 144 + 25 = 169, so the hypotenuse is 13 cm.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, '13', ARRAY['13 cm']::text[], null, null),
('fc90ea29-e61d-400d-9a7e-e09e724ee063', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'For the triangle below, write down the value of tan(θ) as a fraction in simplest form, where θ is the angle at the bottom-left corner.', null, null, 'The tangent of the angle is the opposite side over the adjacent side, which is 6 ÷ 8 = 3/4.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, '3/4', ARRAY['0.75', '6/8']::text[], null, null),
('037ef60e-9084-4c26-be12-24f00dace567', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The rectangle below has the dimensions shown. Its exact perimeter is', '["√52 cm","10√2 cm","5√2 cm","12√2 cm"]'::jsonb, 1, '√18 = 3√2 and √8 = 2√2. The perimeter is 2(3√2 + 2√2) = 2 × 5√2 = 10√2 cm.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('dc74bd16-56cb-4dba-b4c6-991f9cb43b5c', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The rectangle below has the dimensions shown. Write down its area, in square centimetres.', null, null, 'Area = √12 × √3 = √36 = 6 cm². Alternatively 2√3 × √3 = 2 × 3 = 6.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, '6', ARRAY['6 cm²', '6 cm2']::text[], null, null),
('80f1d1ef-967b-49e5-82d9-c6da28765200', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The triangle below is enlarged so that every side is three times as long. Its area becomes', '["three times as large","nine times as large","six times as large","twenty-seven times as large"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Area scales with the square of the length factor, so tripling every side multiplies the area by 3² = 9.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('4db75621-c3ae-4bb1-aad3-85661b55a3c2', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The right-angled triangle below has the measurements shown. Write down its area, in square centimetres.', null, null, 'The area of a right-angled triangle is half the base times the height: 0.5 × 9 × 6 = 27 cm².', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, '27', ARRAY['27 cm²', '27 cm2']::text[], null, null),
('876d45c5-c23c-4ec0-bfff-ef973696d779', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'In the triangle below, the angle at the bottom-left corner is closest to', '["30°","60°","90°","45°"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two shorter sides are equal, so the triangle is isosceles and the two non-right angles are equal. They must total 90°, so each is 45°.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('f354aec5-216f-4920-acc2-1a199e21b14f', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'On the number line below, the marked point lies between two consecutive whole numbers. Write down the smaller of those two whole numbers.', null, null, '5² = 25 and 6² = 36, so √30 lies between 5 and 6. The smaller whole number is 5.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, '5', ARRAY['five']::text[], null, null),
('715a86dd-d712-475c-8c6b-c419bacd1c5d', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'The number line below marks the solution of an inequality. That inequality is', '["x ≥ 2","x > 2","x ≤ 2","x = 2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mark sits at 2 and the label shows every value from 2 upwards is included, so the inequality is x ≥ 2.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('5ecb0afd-48bb-40ec-9498-a32eb09ac0a7', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The number line below shows two marked values. Write down the distance between them.', null, null, 'The distance between −6 and 4 is 4 − (−6) = 10 degrees.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, '10', ARRAY['10 °C', '10 degrees']::text[], null, null),
('4b853faa-c6e6-4cca-875d-6d5e5cf5dc37', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The number line below marks the value of 2⁻².', '["−4","4","0.25","0.5"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A negative index gives the reciprocal: 2⁻² = 1 ÷ 2² = 1/4 = 0.25. The mark sits a quarter of the way to 1, which confirms it.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('45b7bc6c-238a-4c6d-95f2-a90d8f76dc1f', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The table below shows the mass of three parcels in scientific notation. Write down the letter of the heaviest parcel.', null, null, 'Comparing the powers of ten first: 10³ is larger than 10² and 10¹, so parcel C at 1100 kg is the heaviest. A is 320 kg and B is 98 kg.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, 'C', ARRAY['Parcel C']::text[], null, null),
('bd82d285-b532-41cd-b3ff-37ddea99e800', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The dot plot below shows the number of goals scored in each of 11 matches. Write down the median.', null, null, 'With 11 values the median is the sixth when they are ordered. Counting along the dot plot, the sixth value is 2.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, '2', null, null, null),
('5a126e56-85b2-4787-a6f5-a083f877d952', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'For the dot plot below, write down the range.', null, null, 'The range is the largest value minus the smallest: 6 − 0 = 6.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, '6', ARRAY['6 goals']::text[], null, null),
('7e613978-1451-496b-b725-ae7ffed4fc85', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'For the dot plot below, which statement is correct?', '["The distribution is symmetric","The distribution is negatively skewed","The mode is 4","The distribution is positively skewed with a possible outlier at 6"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The dots bunch at the low end and tail off to the right, which is positive skew, and the lone value at 6 sits well clear of the rest. The mode is 2, which occurs three times.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('03072ce3-2e91-48a3-ac19-427203a6d4ed', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The table below shows the results of a survey. Write down how many people were surveyed altogether.', null, null, 'Adding every cell gives 24 + 16 + 31 + 29 = 100 people.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, '100', ARRAY['100 people']::text[], null, null),
('760feace-43cb-46e2-a59b-d616d804eed3', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'For the survey below, the proportion of people under 18 who answered Yes is', '["0.24","0.6","0.44","0.4"]'::jsonb, 1, 'There are 24 + 16 = 40 people under 18, of whom 24 answered Yes. That is 24 ÷ 40 = 0.6. Option B divides by everyone surveyed instead.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('f3c99053-9331-4e7f-8666-698ff81b3a93', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The table below gives a five-number summary. Write down the interquartile range.', null, null, 'The interquartile range is Q3 − Q1 = 23 − 11 = 12.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, '12', null, null, null),
('176d85db-da14-43d3-b899-adee4b7a6b81', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Using the five-number summary below, any value greater than which number would be shown as an outlier?', '["35","46","41","29"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The interquartile range is 23 − 11 = 12, and 1.5 × 12 = 18. The upper fence is 23 + 18 = 41, so anything above 41 is an outlier. The maximum of 31 is therefore not one.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('c60421ff-c3ad-4509-93b2-b2dd9ad14515', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The box plots below compare test scores for two classes. Write down the median score for Class B.', null, null, 'The line inside the Class B box marks the median, which sits at 66.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '66', null, null, null),
('4dba5c3b-a9c8-4465-aa63-561694b8a9d0', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'From the box plots below, which statement is correct?', '["Class A had the higher median","Class A had a much larger interquartile range","Class B scored higher on average and had a similar spread","The two classes had identical ranges"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Class B has the higher median, 66 against 58. Both interquartile ranges are 22 and 23, and the ranges are 56 and 54, so the spreads are close.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('368d68ad-5198-467c-b67a-bd97f43e1795', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'For the box plots below, write down the interquartile range for Class A.', null, null, 'The box runs from Q1 = 48 to Q3 = 70, so the interquartile range is 70 − 48 = 22.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '22', null, null, null),
('36cb4e6a-faf3-4ce4-a163-373eb97576a6', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The table below shows the marks of 40 students. Write down the mean mark, correct to one decimal place.', null, null, 'The total of all marks is 5 × 4 + 6 × 9 + 7 × 12 + 8 × 10 + 9 × 5 = 20 + 54 + 84 + 80 + 45 = 283. Dividing by 40 gives 7.075, which is 7.1 to one decimal place.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '7.1', ARRAY['7.075']::text[], null, null),
('2e158e7e-5a96-411b-b819-0427b0b686d2', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'For the frequency table below, the modal mark is', '["7","12","8","9"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mode is the mark occurring most often. The highest frequency is 12, which belongs to a mark of 7. Option B is the frequency rather than the mark.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('552280cb-57f3-4045-91fa-013121c3ec1c', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The two-way table below shows survey results. Write down the percentage of students who cycle to school, correct to one decimal place.', null, null, 'Altogether 18 + 24 = 42 students cycle, out of 18 + 32 + 45 + 24 + 28 + 53 = 200. That is 42 ÷ 200 = 0.21, which is 21.0%.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '21.0', ARRAY['21', '21%']::text[], null, null),
('4714e513-6579-4a12-ab30-30d4d4e5d733', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'From the table below, the probability that a randomly chosen Year 10 student catches the bus is closest to', '["0.265","0.490","0.535","0.505"]'::jsonb, 3, 'There are 24 + 28 + 53 = 105 Year 10 students, of whom 53 catch the bus. That is 53 ÷ 105 = 0.505. Option B divides by all 200 students instead.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('20684ed8-40d7-4380-afc0-554a9264eb50', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The bar chart below shows sales for four months. Write down the total number of units sold.', null, null, 'Adding the four bars gives 120 + 95 + 140 + 165 = 520 units.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '520', ARRAY['520 units']::text[], null, null),
('534e2317-571d-4b27-967d-ab167d3b7ea9', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'From the bar chart below, the percentage increase from February to April is closest to', '["42.4%","73.7%","70.0%","57.6%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The increase is 165 − 95 = 70. As a percentage of February, 70 ÷ 95 = 0.737, so 73.7%. Option B divides by April instead of February.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('63235f4b-f2c6-4b1f-9082-5b4d6ed5c77b', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The bar chart below shows sales for four months. Write down the mean monthly sales.', null, null, 'The four months total 520 units, so the mean is 520 ÷ 4 = 130 units.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '130', ARRAY['130 units']::text[], null, null),
('8b060f9f-516a-4454-9dad-a00fad095e55', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The table below shows the height and arm span of five students, in centimetres. Write down the number of students whose arm span exceeds their height.', null, null, 'Comparing each pair, A gains 3 cm, C gains 2 cm and D gains 4 cm, while B and E are shorter in arm span than in height. Three students exceed.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '3', null, null, null),
('5ebac843-61aa-4116-807f-026fb5412890', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Based on the table below, the association between height and arm span is best described as', '["strong and negative","strong and positive","weak and positive","no association"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taller students consistently have longer arm spans, and the two measures stay within a few centimetres of each other, which indicates a strong positive association.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('be8dd834-8cfc-4954-92ee-22143e265f17', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The graph below models a car’s value over time. Write down its value, in dollars, when new.', null, null, 'When new the car is at t = 0, where the curve starts. The graph shows a value of $28 000.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, '28000', ARRAY['$28 000', '28 000', '$28000']::text[], null, null),
('70f9682d-95c4-4ce9-bf1a-e700cfddf15d', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'From the graph below, the car’s value after 4 years is closest to', '["$11 200","$18 900","$9500","$14 600"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reading the curve at 4 years gives about $14 600. Checking the model, 28 000 × 0.85⁴ = $14 616. Option B assumes a flat 15% of the original each year.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('0c836cf2-8bb6-49ce-bc57-3fb347183324', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The graph below shows two mobile plans. Write down the number of minutes at which they cost the same.', null, null, 'The two lines cross where 20 + 0.08m = 40 + 0.02m, so 0.06m = 20 and m = 333 minutes to the nearest whole minute.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, '333', ARRAY['333 minutes', '334', '333.3']::text[], null, null),
('d03af15c-378d-4d06-ae1e-5002cbbaecc6', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'From the graph below, a customer who uses 200 minutes a month should choose', '["Plan A, because it is cheaper below about 333 minutes","Plan B, because it has the lower rate per minute","either, because they cost the same","Plan B, because it is cheaper at every usage level"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 200 minutes Plan A costs 20 + 16 = $36 and Plan B costs 40 + 4 = $44. Plan B only becomes cheaper beyond the crossing point at about 333 minutes.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('49632d87-b644-4217-95c0-a708ac19cfcd', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The graph below shows y = x² − 6x + 5. Write down the larger of its two x-intercepts.', null, null, 'Factorising gives (x − 1)(x − 5) = 0, so the intercepts are at x = 1 and x = 5. The larger is 5, which matches the graph.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, '5', ARRAY['x = 5']::text[], null, null),
('93c05d1e-bc40-4b47-a993-6b23e762768f', 'algebra_equations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'From the graph below, the minimum value of y is', '["−6","3","−4","0"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The turning point sits halfway between the intercepts at x = 3, where y = 9 − 18 + 5 = −4.', 'AC9M10A01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('c679fdc6-f257-49c2-93ff-d54952da92a9', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The right-angled triangle below has the measurements shown. Write down the length of the third side, in centimetres, correct to one decimal place.', null, null, 'By Pythagoras, the missing side squared is 12.4² − 7.5² = 153.76 − 56.25 = 97.51, so the side is 9.9 cm.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, '9.9', ARRAY['9.9 cm']::text[], null, null),
('bd7e1310-91e3-43d8-a9c6-847055ce0a0b', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'In the triangle below, the angle between the 7.5 cm side and the hypotenuse is closest to', '["37.1°","40.9°","49.1°","52.9°"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The angle has adjacent 7.5 and hypotenuse 12.4, so its cosine is 7.5 ÷ 12.4 = 0.6048 and the angle is 52.9°.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('b5affe9e-eacc-444a-84e0-3fe43bbabddd', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'A rectangular paddock has the dimensions shown below. Write down the length of fencing needed to enclose it, in metres.', null, null, 'The perimeter is 2 × (84 + 56) = 2 × 140 = 280 metres.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, '280', ARRAY['280 m']::text[], null, null),
('5e0e0a47-3d46-4d34-905d-78c3b93a201f', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'For the paddock below, the cost of fencing at $37.50 per metre is', '["$5250","$10 500","$176 400","$8400"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The perimeter is 280 m, so the cost is 280 × 37.50 = $10 500. Option C multiplies the area by the rate instead of the perimeter.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('dce4ad4d-32ab-4854-b40d-be78598b6437', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The rectangle below is the floor of a room. Write down its area, in square metres.', null, null, 'The area is 4.8 × 3.5 = 16.8 square metres.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, '16.8', ARRAY['16.8 m²', '16.8 m2']::text[], null, null),
('0ad54e89-ea69-4c1c-b606-3177560b207d', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Carpet for the room below costs $68 per square metre. The cost to carpet it is', '["$1128.00","$564.40","$1142.40","$2284.80"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The floor area is 16.8 m², so the cost is 16.8 × 68 = $1142.40.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('a465c79c-1d4d-445f-9c5b-3e5453ec264a', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The table below shows a savings balance growing at a fixed annual rate. Write down the balance after year 3, to the nearest dollar.', null, null, 'Each year the balance is multiplied by 5300 ÷ 5000 = 1.06, a 6% rate. So year 3 is 5618 × 1.06 = $5955 to the nearest dollar.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, '5955', ARRAY['$5955']::text[], null, null),
('cd548126-4893-4414-b12d-b5ddcf12c7c1', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'From the table below, the annual interest rate is', '["6%","5%","3%","6.36%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The balance rises from 5000 to 5300 in the first year, an increase of 300, which is 300 ÷ 5000 = 6%. Each later year multiplies by the same 1.06.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('cd3abaa3-d5d2-4b9d-82e4-eb9afadc4e72', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The table below shows distances in scientific notation. Write down how many times further the Moon is than the mountain, to the nearest whole number.', null, null, 'Dividing gives (3.8 × 10⁸) ÷ (8.8 × 10³) = 4.3182 × 10⁴, which is 43 182 to the nearest whole number.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, '43182', ARRAY['43182', '4.3 × 10⁴']::text[], null, null),
('ba912c14-5863-4704-b50e-55670d346a0f', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The dot plot below shows the ages of children at a party. Write down the mode.', null, null, 'The mode is the value occurring most often. Age 9 appears four times, more than any other age.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, '9', ARRAY['9 years']::text[], null, null),
('6fa0a5e4-1f97-4757-9053-e10293a6a0a1', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'For the dot plot below, write down the number of children at the party.', null, null, 'Counting every dot gives 1 + 2 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 10 children.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, false, true, '10', ARRAY['10 children']::text[], null, null),
('bb4727d8-613c-4496-9c1b-93f50e62066c', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The right-angled triangle below has the measurements shown. Write down the length of the third side, in centimetres.', null, null, 'By Pythagoras, the missing side squared is 25² − 24² = 625 − 576 = 49, so the side is 7 cm.', 'AC9M10M01', null, false, true, '7', ARRAY['7 cm']::text[], null, null),
('dd7fefc0-ca0c-4890-8cd5-d0772bcfd216', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The table below shows three numbers written as powers. Write down the letter of the largest.', null, null, 'Evaluating each gives 2⁵ = 32, 3³ = 27 and 5² = 25, so A is the largest. A bigger base does not always win — the index matters more here.', 'AC9M10N01', null, false, true, 'A', ARRAY['2⁵', '32']::text[], null, null),
('878bfff9-aa92-41cd-a581-e6e435c5d28d', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'The box plot below summarises delivery times. Write down the range, in minutes.', null, null, 'The range is the largest value minus the smallest: 72 − 12 = 60 minutes.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '60', ARRAY['60 minutes']::text[], null, null),
('b81db955-dceb-408a-85b5-6e4198155dab', 'statistics_probability', 'year_10', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'For the box plot below, write down the interquartile range, in minutes.', null, null, 'The box runs from Q1 = 24 to Q3 = 48, so the interquartile range is 48 − 24 = 24 minutes.', 'AC9M10ST01', null, true, true, '24', ARRAY['24 minutes']::text[], null, null),
('720493f8-914e-4c6f-8a6d-de1224fdafff', 'number_operations', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The table below shows a town’s population growing at a fixed annual rate. Write down the population at the end of year 3, to the nearest whole number.', null, null, 'Each year the population is multiplied by 12 600 ÷ 12 000 = 1.05, a rate of 5%. So year 3 is 13 230 × 1.05 = 13 891.5, which is 13 892 to the nearest whole number.', 'AC9M10N01', null, true, true, '13892', ARRAY['13 892', '13891']::text[], null, null),
('43f64dac-68dc-4596-9798-f72d3dcd5b90', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_10', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The right-angled triangle below has the measurements shown. Write down the size of the angle at the bottom-left corner, in degrees, correct to one decimal place.', null, null, 'The angle has an opposite side of 11 and an adjacent side of 15, so its tangent is 11 ÷ 15 = 0.7333 and the angle is 36.3 degrees.', 'AC9M10M01', null, true, true, '36.3', ARRAY['36.3°']::text[], null, null),
('fdfbf2a5-6bb8-4175-9487-010a9d2aee41', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The thermometer shows the temperature in Thredbo at 6 am.
By 2 pm the temperature had risen by 11 degrees.
What was the temperature at 2 pm?', '["7 °C","−7 °C","15 °C","−15 °C"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The thermometer reads −4 °C. Rising 11 degrees: −4 + 11 = 7, so it was 7 °C. Answering −15 °C means subtracting instead of adding.', 'AC9M8N03', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('f1e80e07-ab41-431b-a765-f7e6f56b54d1', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which number line shows the correct positions of −1.5, 1/4 and 1.75?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 2, '−1.5 is halfway between −2 and −1, 1/4 is a quarter of the way from 0 to 1, and 1.75 is three quarters of the way from 1 to 2. Only number line C has all three in the right place.', 'AC9M8N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('056c1fdd-648f-49bf-a3d1-7b822455fcc0', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'Mia''s bank account was overdrawn. Her balance was −$35.
She then deposited $120.
What is her balance now?', null, null, 'Starting at −35 and adding 120: −35 + 120 = 85, so her balance is $85.', 'AC9M8N03', null, false, true, '$85', ARRAY['85']::text[], null, null),
('35b1c0b8-7dd8-46d6-9295-d171cf9a426a', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these is equal to 2⁵ × 2³?', '["2⁸","2¹⁵","4⁸","4¹⁵"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When powers of the same base are multiplied, the indices are added, so 2⁵ × 2³ is 2⁸. Multiplying the indices gives 2¹⁵, which is a common mistake.', 'AC9M8N02', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('1092d62e-c9de-41b4-b48d-ee52606cabfa', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the value of (−3)² − 3²?', '["−18","12","18","0"]'::jsonb, 3, '(−3)² is 9, because a negative times a negative is positive, and 3² is also 9. So the value is 9 − 9 = 0.', 'AC9M8N02', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('52fb9f1a-0d0a-4fb7-8ccd-0694eac897fe', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Ava buys the two items shown.
She pays with a $50 note.
How much change should she get?', '["$17.55","$18.65","$19.65","$31.35"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The items cost $12.95 + $18.40 = $31.35 altogether. Her change is $50.00 − $31.35 = $18.65. $31.35 is the cost of the items, not the change.', 'AC9M8N03', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('91f6affd-f10f-4dd8-8077-4453e34a5118', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'A recipe for 6 muffins uses 150 g of butter.
How many grams of butter are needed for 15 muffins?', null, null, 'Each muffin needs 150 ÷ 6 = 25 g of butter, so 15 muffins need 15 × 25 = 375 g.', 'AC9M8N05', null, false, true, '375 g', ARRAY['375']::text[], null, null),
('3efff252-5ed7-4ca7-849d-f9786c6d3e51', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Tom and Lily share $48 in the ratio 3 : 5, as the bar model shows.
How much does Lily get?', '["$16","$18","$24","$30"]'::jsonb, 3, 'There are 3 + 5 = 8 equal parts, so each part is $48 ÷ 8 = $6. Lily has 5 parts, which is $30. $18 is what Tom gets.', 'AC9M8N05', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('716c69dc-d76f-47f9-b730-e84bb9e6d514', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'A jacket normally costs $60.
It is on sale for 15% off.
What is the sale price?', '["$9","$45","$57","$51"]'::jsonb, 3, '10% of $60 is $6 and 5% is $3, so the discount is $9. The sale price is $60 − $9 = $51. $9 is the discount, not the price.', 'AC9M8N04', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('ef89ece8-348b-4617-884e-a1e168784a56', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these fractions is equal to a decimal that repeats forever?', '["3/8","7/20","2/9","9/25"]'::jsonb, 2, '2/9 = 0.222… with the 2 repeating forever. The others stop: 3/8 = 0.375, 7/20 = 0.35 and 9/25 = 0.36. A fraction in simplest form stops only when its denominator has no prime factors other than 2 and 5.', 'AC9M8N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('80eebf16-daab-466e-a058-46cc073490d7', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which number is closest to the position of the arrow?', '["√60","√40","√50","√70"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The arrow is at about 7.75, and 7.75 × 7.75 is about 60. Check the others: √50 is just above 7, √40 is below 7 and √70 is above 8.', 'AC9M8N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('197679ca-879f-43cb-9c8f-32d51e8da858', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'foundation', 'short_answer', 'A 2-litre bottle of juice is poured into glasses.
Each glass holds 250 mL.
How many glasses can be filled?', null, null, '2 litres is 2000 mL, and 2000 ÷ 250 = 8, so 8 glasses can be filled.', 'AC9M8N03', null, false, true, '8 glasses', ARRAY['8']::text[], null, null),
('45dd26c2-15b3-42d2-8c59-8d84aca70c60', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The price list shows four sizes of rice.
Which size is the best value?', '["500 g bag","1 kg bag","5 kg bag","2 kg bag"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Compare the price per kilogram: the 500 g bag works out at $5.00 per kg, the 1 kg bag at $4.40, the 2 kg bag at $4.00 and the 5 kg bag at $4.20. The 2 kg bag is cheapest per kilogram. The biggest bag is not always the best value.', 'AC9M8N05', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('14f18da7-5895-43dc-a7cb-262fe74f09b1', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'What is the value of 5⁰ + 5¹ + 5²?', null, null, 'Any non-zero number to the power 0 is 1, so 5⁰ is 1. Then 5¹ is 5 and 5² is 25. The total is 1 + 5 + 25 = 31.', 'AC9M8N02', null, false, true, '31', null, null, null),
('92802335-c0c3-49f5-9cd6-f4f1ac167fe3', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows the change in the number of cars in a car park each hour.
There were 120 cars in the car park at 9 am.
How many cars were there at 1 pm?', '["114","124","134","236"]'::jsonb, 1, '120 + 38 − 15 + 22 − 41 = 124. Treating every change as an increase gives 236.', 'AC9M8N03', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('5b879cc0-35a4-4dd6-bb86-f24dd4544e9c', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which list is in order from smallest to largest?', '["0.4, 3/8, 0.45, 1/2","0.4, 0.45, 3/8, 1/2","3/8, 0.45, 0.4, 1/2","3/8, 0.4, 0.45, 1/2"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Write each as a decimal: 3/8 = 0.375 and 1/2 = 0.5. In order: 0.375, 0.4, 0.45, 0.5.', 'AC9M8N01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('3d6377cc-9ee2-4bc8-b6a9-58bf608b71f6', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which expression is the same as 3(2x + 5)?', '["6x + 5","5x + 8","6x + 8","6x + 15"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Multiply each term inside the brackets by 3: 3 × 2x is 6x and 3 × 5 = 15, giving 6x + 15. 6x + 5 forgets to multiply the 5.', 'AC9M8A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('e1be3581-dbd0-44e2-bdeb-a4840dad0baf', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The rectangle has the side lengths shown.
Which expression gives its perimeter?', '["3x + 3","6x + 3","6x + 6","2x² + 3x"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The perimeter is two lengths plus two widths: 2(2x + 3) + 2x = 4x + 6 + 2x = 6x + 6. 2x² + 3x is the area, not the perimeter.', 'AC9M8A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('cd5b52f8-420a-4c9b-a1d4-154b463960cc', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'Solve the equation 4m − 7 = 29.', null, null, 'Add 7 to both sides to get 4m = 36, then divide both sides by 4 to get m = 9. Check: 4 × 9 − 7 = 29.', 'AC9M8A03', null, false, true, '9', ARRAY['m = 9', 'm=9']::text[], null, null),
('8163c789-a5b0-4547-8433-56036421f2a0', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Priya makes this pattern with tiles.
Which rule gives the number of tiles, t, in Design n?', '["t = 3n + 2","t = n + 4","t = 2n + 3","t = 5n"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The designs have 5, 8 and 11 tiles, going up by 3 each time, so the rule starts with 3n. For Design 1, 3 × 1 = 3 and 2 more makes 5, so t = 3n + 2. The rule t = 5n works for Design 1 only.', 'AC9M8A04', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('f7c7f167-bf56-4bd0-8516-390f6852baa9', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which rule matches all the points plotted on the grid?', '["y = x + 1","y = 2x + 1","y = 2x − 1","y = x + 2"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each time x goes up by 1, y goes up by 2, and when x is 0, y is 1. So the rule is y = 2x + 1. Check the point (3, 7): 2 × 3 + 1 = 7.', 'AC9M8A03', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('631ecfcf-5264-4cf6-9097-4abf53739b68', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The cost of a taxi ride is C = 2.5k + 4, where C is the cost in dollars and k is the distance in kilometres.
Joel’s ride cost $29.
How far did he travel?', null, null, '29 = 2.5k + 4, so 2.5k = 25 and k = 10. Check: 2.5 × 10 + 4 = 29.', 'AC9M8A02', null, false, true, '10 km', ARRAY['10']::text[], null, null),
('4311e656-5487-420b-ba56-906240845d3a', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which inequality is shown on the number line?', '["x ≥ 3","x > 3","x < 3","x ≤ 3"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The line is shaded from 3 to the right, so x is 3 or more. The filled circle means 3 itself is included, so it is x ≥ 3. An open circle would mean x > 3.', 'AC9M8A03', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('47ddc899-07c7-4743-850e-37c5428b6290', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which expression is the same as 12a − 18?', '["6(2a − 3)","6(2a − 18)","3(4a − 18)","12(a − 6)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The highest common factor of 12a and 18 is 6, so 12a − 18 = 6(2a − 3). Expanding 6(2a − 3) gives back 12a − 18.', 'AC9M8A01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('fadf37a2-f04a-4e97-9ab1-9a1a4a47fae8', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows values of x and y.
Which rule connects x and y?', '["y = x + 6","y = 3x + 4","y = 4x + 3","y = 7x"]'::jsonb, 1, 'y goes up by 3 each time x goes up by 1, so the rule starts with 3x. When x = 1, 3 × 1 = 3 and y is 7, so add 4: y = 3x + 4. The rule y = x + 6 only works when x is 1.', 'AC9M8A04', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('737e6c20-2a9d-486c-bb54-07901f051a5e', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Kai thinks of a number.
He multiplies it by 3, then subtracts 5.
The answer is 4 more than the number he first thought of.
What was his number?', '["1.5","3","4.5","9"]'::jsonb, 2, 'If the number is n, then 3n − 5 = n + 4. Subtracting n from both sides gives 2n − 5 = 4, so 2n = 9 and n = 4.5. Check: 3 × 4.5 − 5 = 8.5, which is 4 more than 4.5.', 'AC9M8A03', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('9d44071c-affe-49b3-be41-701eff5ffc67', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Find the value of 5a − 2b when a = 4 and b = −3.', null, null, '5a is 20 and 2b is −6. Subtracting a negative is the same as adding, so 20 + 6 = 26.', 'AC9M8A02', null, false, true, '26', null, null, null),
('4836ee34-13f5-440f-971b-820046237433', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Lines AB and CD are parallel.
What is the size of angle x?', null, null, 'The angles marked 51° and x are co-interior: they sit between the parallel lines on the same side of the line crossing them, so they add to 180°. x = 180 − 51 = 129.', 'AC9M7SP03', null, false, true, '129°', ARRAY['129']::text[], null, null),
('3ae90ed8-056a-4076-86b2-28b5fa6e0480', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the size of angle y?', '["65°","105°","125°","115°"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles: y = 48 + 67 = 115°. Another way: the third angle inside is 180 − 48 − 67 = 65°, and y = 180 − 65 = 115°.', 'AC9M7SP02', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('b661611d-864f-4b19-b7a7-660a628ae201', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the area of the parallelogram?', '["60 cm²","30 cm²","34 cm²","72 cm²"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The area of a parallelogram is base × perpendicular height: 12 × 5 = 60 cm². Using the slanted side gives 12 × 6 = 72, which is too big, and 34 cm is the perimeter.', 'AC9M8M01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('d09fc115-d213-4c08-b985-fc35d7867835', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'A garden bed is in the shape of a trapezium.
What is its area?', null, null, 'The area of a trapezium is half the sum of the parallel sides times the height. Half of (8 + 14) is 11, and 11 × 6 = 66, so the area is 66 m².', 'AC9M8M01', null, false, true, '66 m²', ARRAY['66']::text[], null, null),
('3ccdcefb-3002-42ae-8fe7-8f921b99e110', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'What is the area of the shaded shape?', null, null, 'Split the shape into pieces. The rectangle along the bottom is 6 × 3 = 18 cm². The rectangle at the top left is 3 × 2 = 6 cm². The triangle at the top right is half of a 3 by 2 rectangle, which is 3 cm². Total: 18 + 6 + 3 = 27 cm².', 'AC9M8M01', null, false, true, '27 cm²', ARRAY['27']::text[], null, null),
('b5a3c014-959f-4b5b-a5d8-8d2ce15b16e1', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'A box is shaped like a rectangular prism.
What is its volume?', '["17 cm³","60 cm³","164 cm³","120 cm³"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Volume = length × width × height = 10 × 4 × 3 = 120 cm³. 164 cm² is its surface area, and 17 is the three lengths added together.', 'AC9M8M02', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('ecefd9b2-540d-44dc-bac6-449626e37023', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which net can be folded to make a cube?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 1, 'Net B folds into a cube: the row of four wraps around, and the squares above and below become the top and bottom. Net A has five squares in a row, net C contains a 2 by 2 block of squares, and in net D both extra squares fold onto the same face.', 'AC9M8SP01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('9ae8f2b6-5ac8-4be4-a61c-faad361f2077', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which statement is always true for a rhombus?', '["All its angles are 90°.","All four sides are equal in length.","Its diagonals are equal in length.","It has exactly one pair of parallel sides."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A rhombus is a quadrilateral with four equal sides. Its angles are 90° and its diagonals are equal only when it is also a square, and it always has two pairs of parallel sides.', 'AC9M8SP02', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('d5ed9e60-2ddc-4795-bc91-3e01b3d98f36', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Triangle A is transformed to triangle B.
Which transformation was used?', '["A reflection in the y-axis","A reflection in the x-axis","A rotation of 180° about the origin","A translation 5 units to the left"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every point of A keeps its y-coordinate and has its x-coordinate change sign: (1, 1) goes to (−1, 1) and (4, 1) goes to (−4, 1). That is a reflection in the y-axis. A translation 5 units left would send (1, 1) to (−4, 1) and leave the triangle facing the same way.', 'AC9M8SP03', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('f20720e1-d61b-4c5e-8970-89eb86e43d70', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Use the train timetable.
How long does the train take to travel from Central to Katoomba?', '["1 h 53 min","2 h 47 min","3 h 7 min","2 h 7 min"]'::jsonb, 3, 'From 14:48 to 15:48 is 1 hour, and from 15:48 to 16:55 is another 1 hour 7 minutes, so the trip takes 2 hours 7 minutes. 1 h 53 min comes from subtracting the minutes the wrong way round.', 'AC9M8M04', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('99e9425b-cc91-48c4-a166-632ab317fe52', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the size of the angle shown on the protractor?', '["55°","115°","125°","135°"]'::jsonb, 2, 'One arm of the angle lies along 0° on the outer scale, so read the outer scale where the other arm crosses it: 125°. Reading the inner scale by mistake gives 55°.', 'AC9M7M03', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('a1ea0b03-b73f-43a1-9e66-f7fb6f26fad5', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'A television screen is 48 cm high and 64 cm wide.
How long is the diagonal of the screen?', null, null, 'The diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with sides of 48 cm and 64 cm. 48 × 48 is 2304 and 64 × 64 is 4096, and 2304 + 4096 = 6400. The square root of 6400 is 80, so the diagonal is 80 cm.', 'AC9M8M06', null, false, true, '80 cm', ARRAY['80']::text[], null, null),
('05082640-ba80-4059-af89-7ce2b0f33673', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The stack is made of identical cubes with no gaps.
How many cubes are in the stack?', '["8","9","10","12"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Count each column: the back row has 3, 2 and 1 cubes and the front row has 2, 1 and 1. 3 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 10. Some cubes at the back are hidden, but they must be there to hold up the cubes on top.', 'AC9M8M02', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('401ad1bb-1ce0-4d84-b184-fb18f325ffb4', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Lucas spins the arrow on this spinner once.
What is the probability that it stops on blue?', '["1/8","1/4","3/8","3/5"]'::jsonb, 2, '3 of the 8 equal sections are blue, so the probability is 3/8. The answer 3/5 compares blue with the other sections instead of with all 8.', 'AC9M8P01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('c4343985-912f-41cc-b3b7-99ea9031d919', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The dot plot shows the number of goals a netball team scored in each game of its season.
What is the median number of goals?', null, null, 'There are 10 games. Put in order, the 5th and 6th values are both 21, so the median is 21.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, false, true, '21', null, null, null),
('07e63840-5f84-4ad5-9361-c5242839fea9', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The Venn diagram shows the students in a class who play soccer and who play basketball.
How many students play soccer but not basketball?', '["4","13","20","9"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Students who play soccer but not basketball are inside the soccer circle but outside the overlap: 9. The answer 13 also counts the 4 who play both.', 'AC9M8P01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('f76a5b5b-6f80-4da2-a8d8-d28a98685747', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows how some students travel to school.
One of these students is chosen at random.
What is the probability that the student is in Year 8 and walks to school?', '["3/16","3/8","5/9","1/2"]'::jsonb, 0, '15 of the 80 students are in Year 8 and walk, and 15/80 = 3/16. The answer 3/8 is 15/40, the probability for a student already known to be in Year 8.', 'AC9M8P01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('916d42dd-4584-4cbe-8200-c3e5519a42a1', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The graph shows how the students in Year 8 travel to school.
How many more students walk than ride a bike?', '["15","20","25","45"]'::jsonb, 2, '35 students walk and 10 ride a bike, so 35 − 10 = 25 more students walk.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('c02aae4c-5831-4edf-959a-b734aeff78e5', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A council wants to know what all its residents think about building a new skate park.
Which survey is most likely to give a fair picture of residents’ views?', '["Asking people at the existing skate park","Asking a random sample of residents from across the whole council area","Asking the council’s own staff","Putting a poll on the skate park’s social media page"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A random sample from the whole area gives every resident a chance of being asked. The other surveys mostly reach people who already have a particular view, such as skaters or council workers.', 'AC9M8ST01', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('97f47433-da13-4f3d-8ba9-48a3e1c87361', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The ages of five people in a lift are 34, 36, 38, 41 and 41.
A 90-year-old gets into the lift.
Which statement is true?', '["The mean increases by more than the median does.","The median increases by more than the mean does.","The mean and the median both stay the same.","The mode changes."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Before: the ages add to 190, so the mean is 38, and the median is 38. After: the ages add to 280, so the mean is about 46.7, and the median is halfway between 38 and 41, which is 39.5. An extreme value pulls the mean far more than the median. The mode stays 41.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, false, true, null, null, null, null),
('07f1a37d-1be5-4f6e-b34a-149edcde5bc0', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The stem-and-leaf plot shows the scores of a class in a quiz.
What is the range of the scores?', null, null, 'The lowest score is 35 and the highest is 64, so the range is 64 − 35 = 29.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, false, true, '29', null, null, null),
('34575e86-b9d7-42ae-a93e-a916bfdd2e69', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'A mechanic’s bill is $385 before GST.
GST of 10% is added.
What is the total bill?', null, null, '10% of $385 is $38.50, so the total bill is $385 + $38.50 = $423.50.', 'AC9M8N04', null, true, true, '$423.50', ARRAY['423.50', '423.5']::text[], null, null),
('549627fb-dc4e-4a74-a9ac-86a17c54c6d6', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Zoe’s supermarket receipt is torn.
She remembers that she paid $47.60 altogether.
How much did the roast chicken cost?', '["$28.80","$29.80","$30.80","$39.30"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The milk and bread cost $9.30 + $8.50 = $17.80. The chicken cost $47.60 − $17.80 = $29.80. The answer $39.30 subtracts only the bread.', 'AC9M8N03', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('a3325ab4-4b9c-4a9c-8888-40932d9b77cb', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'A house was bought for $640 000 and later sold for $705 000.
What was the percentage increase in its value?
Give your answer correct to one decimal place.', null, null, 'The increase is $65 000. As a percentage of the price it was bought for: 65000 ÷ 640000 × 100 = 10.15625%, which is 10.2% to one decimal place.', 'AC9M8N04', null, true, true, '10.2%', ARRAY['10.2']::text[], null, null),
('71913be7-bc5a-4546-aace-0f7176883862', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A shop buys jackets for $48 each and sells them for $78 each.
What is the profit as a percentage of the cost price?', '["30%","38.5%","62.5%","162.5%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The profit on each jacket is $78 − $48 = $30. As a percentage of the cost price: 30 ÷ 48 × 100 = 62.5%. The answer 38.5% divides by the selling price instead.', 'AC9M8N04', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('1db5eeee-7f5b-4697-9b75-94360dfa3239', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The pie chart shows how Sam spends his monthly income of $1250.
How much does he spend on rent?', '["$500","$400","$525","$750"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rent is 40% of $1250. 10% of $1250 is $125, so 40% is 4 × $125 = $500.', 'AC9M8N04', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('2c98d67f-8f93-4dd5-a877-5d540dba93a6', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'A car travels 342 km in 3 hours 45 minutes.
What is its average speed?', null, null, '45 minutes is 0.75 of an hour, so the time is 3.75 hours. Average speed = 342 ÷ 3.75 = 91.2 km/h. Using 3.45 hours is a common mistake.', 'AC9M8N05', null, true, true, '91.2 km/h', ARRAY['91.2']::text[], null, null),
('ae2ca0ff-a46f-4c51-9cfd-37221d257253', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows the prices of different packs of batteries.
Which pack has the lowest price per battery?', '["4 batteries","10 batteries","16 batteries","24 batteries"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Price per battery: $5.20 ÷ 4 is $1.30, $12.00 ÷ 10 is $1.20, $18.40 ÷ 16 is $1.15 and $28.80 ÷ 24 is $1.20. The pack of 16 is cheapest per battery.', 'AC9M8N05', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('489b7ef3-e9e5-4998-9d46-41d06d42b1d0', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'A nature reserve had 625 koalas.
The number fell by 12% one year, then rose by 12% the next year.
How many koalas were there after the two years?', null, null, 'After the fall: 625 × 0.88 = 550. After the rise: 550 × 1.12 = 616. The number does not return to 625, because the 12% rise is 12% of a smaller number.', 'AC9M8N04', null, true, true, '616', ARRAY['616 koalas']::text[], null, null),
('147d0a66-c22d-4b66-8ee9-7c4d99089fb3', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The scale shows the mass of a bag of apples.
Apples cost $4.90 per kilogram.
How much does the bag of apples cost?', '["$9.80","$12.25","$14.70","$11.76"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The scale reads 2.4 kg, and 2.4 × 4.90 = 11.76, so the apples cost $11.76. $12.25 would be the cost of 2.5 kg.', 'AC9M8N05', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('5ab3ff30-393d-44b4-94fb-5148fc0b3b7b', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'One Australian dollar buys 96 Japanese yen.
How many Australian dollars is 18 000 yen?', '["$18.75","$187.50","$1875.00","$1 728 000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Divide the number of yen by the exchange rate: 18000 ÷ 96 = 187.5, so it is $187.50. Multiplying instead of dividing gives the last option, which is far too large.', 'AC9M8N05', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('bd04e2b0-c7f5-48dc-8dc2-a4443bfc868e', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Ella rides from her home to the library along the dotted route.
How far does she ride?', '["7 km","12.5 km","17.5 km","20 km"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The route goes 4 squares east and then 3 squares north, which is 7 squares. Each square is 2.5 km, and 7 × 2.5 = 17.5 km. The answer 7 km counts squares without using the scale.', 'AC9M8N05', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('6f8fd179-df62-4212-8043-6399109704e4', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which number lies between √75 and √80?', '["8.8","8.5","8.6","9.0"]'::jsonb, 0, '√75 is about 8.66 and √80 is about 8.94. Of the options, only 8.8 lies between them.', 'AC9M8N01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('a5409234-55bc-42e3-959e-0e44d0d74636', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'A school raised $3840 at its fete.
It gave 5/8 of the money to charity and kept the rest.
How much did the school keep?', '["$480","$768","$1440","$2400"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The school kept 3/8 of the money. One eighth of $3840 is $480, so three eighths is 3 × 480 = $1440. $2400 is the amount given to charity.', 'AC9M8N03', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('87c638fb-6c4f-4a33-83f8-6735443fb9bd', 'number_operations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Concrete is made by mixing cement, sand and gravel in the ratio 1 : 2 : 4 by mass.
How many kilograms of sand are in 350 kg of concrete?', null, null, 'There are 1 + 2 + 4 = 7 equal parts, so each part is 350 ÷ 7 = 50 kg. Sand is 2 parts: 2 × 50 = 100 kg.', 'AC9M8N05', null, true, true, '100 kg', ARRAY['100']::text[], null, null),
('70224c76-1c15-486a-a332-6eb2fd872719', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A phone plan costs $15 a month plus 20 cents for each minute of calls.
Which graph shows the monthly cost for up to 100 minutes of calls?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The cost starts at $15 for no calls and rises by $5 every 25 minutes, reaching $35 at 100 minutes. Graph D is the only one that does this: graph A has no monthly fee, graph B ignores the calls and graph C charges 30 cents a minute.', 'AC9M8A04', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('1ffd6505-366d-4fcb-9417-8213d047a0da', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'The formula F = 1.8C + 32 converts a temperature in degrees Celsius (C) to degrees Fahrenheit (F).
What is 37 °C in degrees Fahrenheit?', null, null, 'Substitute C = 37: F = 1.8 × 37 + 32 = 66.6 + 32 = 98.6.', 'AC9M8A02', null, true, true, '98.6', ARRAY['98.6 °F', '98.6°F']::text[], null, null),
('0459d1d3-d469-4ad3-8980-525a77d1ae58', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Aiden has $240 and saves $35 each week.
Bree has $520 and spends $35 each week.
After how many weeks will they have the same amount of money?', '["3","7","8","4"]'::jsonb, 3, 'After w weeks, Aiden has 240 + 35w dollars and Bree has 520 − 35w dollars. Setting these equal gives 70w = 280, so w = 4. Check: 240 + 140 = 380 and 520 − 140 = 380.', 'AC9M8A03', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('73bc8d1f-df26-4333-a7b3-82df2d3e218b', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The graph shows the depth of water in a tank as it is being filled.
Which rule gives the depth, d centimetres, after t minutes?', '["d = 5t + 20","d = 20t + 5","d = 4t + 20","d = 60 − 5t"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The depth starts at 20 cm and rises 40 cm in 8 minutes, which is 5 cm each minute, so d = 5t + 20. The rule d = 20t + 5 swaps the starting depth and the rate.', 'AC9M8A04', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('ebcc4662-c560-41ce-a334-35718bd23032', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The perimeter of a rectangle is 57.6 m.
Its length is three times its width.
What is its width?', '["4.8 m","7.2 m","9.6 m","14.4 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'If the width is w, the length is 3w, and the perimeter is 2(3w + w) = 8w. So 8w = 57.6 and w = 7.2 m. The answer 14.4 m treats the perimeter as four widths.', 'AC9M8A03', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('77d9cd84-7f82-41ab-af93-94a01bf80573', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'A swimming pool is being emptied at a steady rate.
The table shows how much water is left in the pool.
If it keeps emptying at this rate, after how many hours will the pool be empty?', '["12","16","20","24"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The amount of water falls by 2400 litres every hour. 48000 ÷ 2400 = 20, so the pool is empty after 20 hours.', 'AC9M8A04', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('e3ab1797-199a-4c96-8c4a-892edd3c0a16', 'algebra_equations', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The cost, C dollars, of hiring a jumping castle for h hours is C = 85 + 32.5h.
A family paid $247.50.
For how many hours did they hire the castle?', null, null, '247.50 = 85 + 32.5h, so 32.5h = 162.50 and h = 162.50 ÷ 32.5 = 5. Check: 85 + 32.5 × 5 = 247.50.', 'AC9M8A02', null, true, true, '5 hours', ARRAY['5']::text[], null, null),
('d2f2f8eb-86fa-44fd-bff4-8ca42f714d03', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'A circular garden bed has a diameter of 3.6 m.
What is its area, correct to one decimal place?', null, null, 'The radius is half the diameter, 1.8 m. Area = π × 1.8 × 1.8, which is about 10.18, so 10.2 m² to one decimal place. Using the diameter as the radius gives about 40.7 m².', 'AC9M8M03', null, true, true, '10.2 m²', ARRAY['10.2']::text[], null, null),
('01730608-3631-47b1-b538-1b661f726778', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A bicycle wheel has a radius of 34 cm.
How far does the bicycle travel when the wheel turns 50 times?
Give your answer to the nearest metre.', '["53 m","214 m","1068 m","107 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'One turn moves the bicycle one circumference: 2 × π × 34 is about 213.6 cm. Fifty turns is about 10680 cm, which is 106.8 m, or 107 m to the nearest metre. The answer 53 m uses the radius as the diameter.', 'AC9M8M03', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('84dd236b-e80b-4f51-adcd-d83ca3ab085c', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'A 6.5 m ladder leans against a vertical wall.
The foot of the ladder is 1.8 m from the wall.
How far up the wall does the ladder reach, correct to one decimal place?', null, null, 'The ladder is the hypotenuse. 6.5 × 6.5 is 42.25 and 1.8 × 1.8 is 3.24, and 42.25 − 3.24 = 39.01. The square root of 39.01 is about 6.25, so the ladder reaches 6.2 m up the wall. Adding instead of subtracting gives an answer longer than the ladder.', 'AC9M8M06', null, true, true, '6.2 m', ARRAY['6.2']::text[], null, null),
('ed2ecb5a-73a9-4a4b-b03d-80cd5c86900e', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A wheelchair ramp rises 0.9 m over a horizontal distance of 10.8 m.
How long is the sloping surface of the ramp, to the nearest centimetre?', '["9.90 m","10.76 m","10.84 m","11.70 m"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sloping surface is the hypotenuse. 10.8 × 10.8 is 116.64 and 0.9 × 0.9 is 0.81, and 116.64 + 0.81 = 117.45. The square root of 117.45 is about 10.837, which is 10.84 m. 11.70 m just adds the two lengths.', 'AC9M8M06', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('0fce4a8a-616d-4d75-a963-09f3ae8c3eb2', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the volume of the triangular prism?', '["25 cm³","90 cm³","180 cm³","360 cm³"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The triangular face has an area of half of 6 × 4, which is 12 cm². Volume = area of the face × length = 12 × 15 = 180 cm³. Forgetting to halve gives 360 cm³.', 'AC9M8M02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('a79477c9-600e-4f43-847d-180b64dcbb2c', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The jug holds the amount of water shown.
Ravi pours all of it equally into 4 cups.
How many millilitres of water go into each cup?', null, null, 'The jug shows 700 mL, and 700 ÷ 4 = 175, so each cup gets 175 mL.', 'AC9M8M02', null, true, true, '175 mL', ARRAY['175']::text[], null, null),
('4cf1c65b-6b28-484e-8a20-412923bc3b4a', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Tom is fitting skirting board around all the edges of this room.
How many metres of skirting board does he need?', '["25.2 m","21.0 m","34.56 m","38.88 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two sides without labels are 7.2 − 4.8 = 2.4 m and 5.4 − 3.6 = 1.8 m. The perimeter is 7.2 + 3.6 + 2.4 + 1.8 + 4.8 + 5.4 = 25.2 m. The answer 21.0 m leaves out the two short sides, and 34.56 is the area in square metres.', 'AC9M8M01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('bdf487eb-fc1d-4010-abf3-513d6f44cabd', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The clock shows the time a flight leaves Perth, in Perth time.
The flight takes 4 hours 10 minutes.
Sydney time is 2 hours ahead of Perth time.
What is the time in Sydney when the flight lands?', '["1:50 am","3:50 am","5:50 am","6:10 am"]'::jsonb, 2, '11:40 pm plus 4 hours 10 minutes is 3:50 am in Perth. Sydney is 2 hours ahead, so the flight lands at 5:50 am Sydney time. The answer 3:50 am forgets the time difference.', 'AC9M8M04', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('a48f3a40-8eb9-431b-bdf7-d9820743d578', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the size of angle p?', '["73°","83°","93°","97°"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The angles in a quadrilateral add to 360°. p = 360 − 112 − 68 − 97 = 83°.', 'AC9M7SP02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('24952b2a-a19d-44cc-a38e-e01491de7954', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'A kite has diagonals of length 9.6 cm and 14.5 cm.
What is the area of the kite?', null, null, 'The area of a kite is half the product of its diagonals: 9.6 × 14.5 = 139.2, and half of that is 69.6 cm².', 'AC9M8M01', null, true, true, '69.6 cm²', ARRAY['69.6']::text[], null, null),
('3d4fbd4d-3982-42c3-b02b-fcfaaded9808', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Point P is rotated 90° anticlockwise about the origin.
What are the coordinates of its new position?', '["(−1, 3)","(1, −3)","(−3, −1)","(3, −1)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A rotation of 90° anticlockwise about the origin sends the point (x, y) to (−y, x), so P(3, 1) moves to (−1, 3). The answer (1, −3) is a clockwise rotation.', 'AC9M8SP03', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('d98c6c96-b714-439e-8a7b-1a7bf62f904c', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'An aquarium is a rectangular prism 60 cm long, 30 cm wide and 35 cm high.
How many litres of water does it hold when full?', '["6.3 L","63 L","630 L","6300 L"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume = 60 × 30 × 35 = 63000 cm³. Since 1000 cm³ is 1 litre, the aquarium holds 63 litres.', 'AC9M8M02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('a8f13c0b-03ff-4ef7-94b4-b0c46830c6dc', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A scale drawing of a bedroom uses a scale of 1 : 50.
On the drawing, the bedroom is 9.2 cm long.
How long is the real bedroom?', '["0.46 m","46 m","460 m","4.6 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each 1 cm on the drawing stands for 50 cm in real life: 9.2 × 50 = 460 cm, which is 4.6 m. The answer 460 m forgets to change centimetres into metres.', 'AC9M8M05', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('9f1fe616-78a3-4050-b244-89b6cb322c5f', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The shape is made from a rectangle and a semicircle.
What is its area, correct to one decimal place?', '["49.8 cm²","47.9 cm²","59.6 cm²","79.3 cm²"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rectangle is 8 × 5 = 40 cm². The semicircle has a radius of 2.5 cm, so its area is half of π × 2.5 × 2.5, about 9.82 cm². The total is about 49.8 cm². The answer 59.6 cm² uses a whole circle.', 'AC9M8M03', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('b195fab9-9c92-4b94-93cc-e637edc01292', 'geometry_measurement', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Lines AB and CD are parallel.
Find the value of x.', null, null, 'The two marked angles are alternate angles between parallel lines, so they are equal: 2x + 7 = x + 29. Subtracting x from both sides gives x + 7 = 29, so x = 22. Each angle is 51°.', 'AC9M7SP03', null, true, true, '22', ARRAY['x = 22', 'x=22']::text[], null, null),
('ddfc499f-fcd2-4a41-8c24-8108fa641b42', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The graph shows the temperature in Alice Springs during one day.
Between which two times did the temperature rise the most?', '["8 am and 10 am","6 am and 8 am","10 am and 12 pm","12 pm and 2 pm"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The steepest upward part of the graph is between 8 am and 10 am, where the temperature rises from 14 °C to 22 °C, a rise of 8 degrees. The other rises are 6, 5 and 3 degrees.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('0fe146a7-36b0-461d-b371-9406bc4a956e', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The table shows the rainfall in Cairns over six months.
What is the mean monthly rainfall, correct to one decimal place?', null, null, 'The total is 412.6 + 435.8 + 422.1 + 196.4 + 91.2 + 49.5 = 1607.6 mm. The mean is 1607.6 ÷ 6, which is about 267.93, so 267.9 mm.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, true, true, '267.9 mm', ARRAY['267.9']::text[], null, null),
('6a00e745-37e7-4fa5-bb91-bc212f7e26bc', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The graph shows the number of adults and children who visited a museum each month.
In which month did the museum have the most visitors in total?', '["February","January","March","April"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Totals: January 1200 + 800 = 2000, February 900 + 1300 = 2200, March 1400 + 700 = 2100, April 1000 + 1100 = 2100. February has the most. March has the most adults, but not the most visitors.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('2aa4c542-65ee-43b9-b58e-043f0fd75748', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Mei dropped a drawing pin 250 times.
It landed point up 95 times.
Based on her results, what is the best estimate of the probability that the pin lands point up?', '["0.095","0.5","0.62","0.38"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The relative frequency is 95 ÷ 250 = 0.38. The answer 0.62 is the estimate for landing point down, and 0.5 assumes both ways are equally likely, which her results do not show.', 'AC9M8P03', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('276dc6ec-99aa-4e54-a82c-52b6839a2885', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The tally shows how many pets each student in a class has.
What is the mean number of pets per student?', null, null, 'There are 5 + 9 + 7 + 3 + 1 = 25 students. The total number of pets is 0 × 5 + 1 × 9 + 2 × 7 + 3 × 3 + 4 × 1 = 36. The mean is 36 ÷ 25 = 1.44.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, true, true, '1.44', null, null, null),
('da87ccc2-b553-48ca-b745-859f723e57af', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The Venn diagram shows the sports played by 60 Year 8 students.
How many of the students play exactly two of the three sports?', '["12","15","18","25"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Exactly two sports means the regions where two circles overlap, but not the centre where all three overlap: 5 + 4 + 6 = 15. Including the 3 who play all three sports gives 18, which is too many.', 'AC9M8P01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('12ab2bce-89c8-4a0c-9b30-56d59cb462b0', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Two fair dice are rolled and the numbers are added.
The table shows all the possible totals.
What is the probability that the total is 9?', '["1/12","1/6","2/9","1/9"]'::jsonb, 3, 'There are 36 equally likely outcomes and 4 of them give a total of 9: 3 and 6, 4 and 5, 5 and 4, and 6 and 3. So the probability is 4/36, which simplifies to 1/9.', 'AC9M8P01', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('4f36dfc8-9cf5-43cd-97b3-cb62af1b9ef5', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Carla’s scores in five tests were 72, 85, 64, 90 and 79.
What score does she need in her sixth test for her mean to be 80?', '["80","85","90","95"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A mean of 80 over six tests needs a total of 6 × 80 = 480. Her five scores add to 72 + 85 + 64 + 90 + 79 = 390, so she needs 480 − 390 = 90.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('db9311b0-4ba7-471a-8d6e-acef8321be50', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows the fruit sold at a school canteen on Monday.
Which column graph correctly shows the data?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every bar in graph C matches the table: apples 30, bananas 45, pears 15 and oranges 25. Graph A shows 40 bananas, graph B swaps pears and oranges, and graph D shows 35 apples.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('fe67265d-2810-41a7-99f2-057d0cf4994b', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'A bag contains red, blue and green counters in the ratio 2 : 3 : 5.
There are 18 blue counters.
How many counters are in the bag altogether?', null, null, '3 parts are 18 counters, so 1 part is 6 counters. There are 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 parts, and 10 × 6 = 60 counters.', 'AC9M8P01', null, true, true, '60', ARRAY['60 counters']::text[], null, null),
('45c6c152-b368-486c-ab2c-87f38a2e22ac', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'The probability that a spinner lands on red is 0.35.
What is the probability that it does not land on red?', '["0.35","0.53","1.35","0.65"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Landing on red and not landing on red are complementary events, so their probabilities add to 1: 1 − 0.35 = 0.65.', 'AC9M8P02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('0025e8d7-0a35-4112-b2eb-2074d48545e7', 'statistics_probability', 'year_8', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The stem-and-leaf plot shows the heights of the plants in a school garden.
What percentage of the plants are taller than 30 cm?
Give your answer correct to one decimal place.', '["38.5%","46.2%","53.8%","61.5%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'There are 13 plants. Six are taller than 30 cm: 32, 34, 36, 38, 41 and 45. A plant of exactly 30 cm is not taller than 30 cm. 6 ÷ 13 × 100 is about 46.2%. The answer 53.8% wrongly includes the 30 cm plant.', 'AC9M8ST02', null, true, true, null, null, null, null),
('e0008bdf-ab53-4572-aee0-62cb1704d03e', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'What number do these blocks show?', '["274","247","724","2047"]'::jsonb, 1, 'There are 2 hundreds, 4 tens and 7 ones, which make 247. 274 swaps the tens and the ones.', 'AC9M4N01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('3d9899c4-52d0-4a3b-a7bd-f73af88132ac', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'Ben has this money in his wallet.
How much money does he have?', null, null, 'The notes make $10 + $5 = $15, the dollar coins make $3, and the other coins make 70 cents. Altogether that is $18.70.', 'AC9M4N08', null, null, true, '$18.70', ARRAY['18.70', '18.7']::text[], null, null),
('949c2a25-23da-4eaa-9864-070d6f70daa0', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which number is four thousand and sixty-two?', '["462","4062","4602","40 062"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Four thousand is 4000 and sixty-two is 62, so the number is 4062. There are no hundreds, so the hundreds digit is 0.', 'AC9M4N01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('417afdcf-1d36-4c2e-9ec5-9d3f5bd4214b', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which number is shown by the arrow?', '["307","357","370","375"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each mark on the number line is 10 more than the one before. The arrow is 7 marks past 300, so it shows 370.', 'AC9M4N01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('7be5f22c-2a5e-43cd-bb8e-3f5ba2a20bce', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Isla buys the book and the pencil.
She pays with a $20 note.
How much change does she get?', '["$6.30","$6.70","$7.30","$13.70"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The book and pencil cost $12.50 + $1.20 = $13.70. The change from $20 is $20.00 − $13.70 = $6.30. $13.70 is the cost, not the change.', 'AC9M4N08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('04440502-7593-4cab-a808-681c3d62e7a3', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'The picture shows the chairs set out for a school concert.
How many chairs are there?', null, null, 'There are 6 rows with 8 chairs in each row, and 6 × 8 = 48.', 'AC9M4N06', null, null, true, '48 chairs', ARRAY['48']::text[], null, null),
('36925904-3417-414d-959a-50bb5957a289', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Mr Lee shares 72 stickers equally among 8 students.
How many stickers does each student get?', null, null, '72 shared into 8 equal groups is 9 in each group, because 8 × 9 = 72.', 'AC9M4N07', null, null, true, '9 stickers', ARRAY['9']::text[], null, null),
('fe3778d8-105b-4057-942e-4fc3f736c4c9', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What fraction of the circle is shaded?', '["3/5","5/8","3/8","8/3"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The circle has 8 equal parts and 3 are shaded, so 3/8 is shaded. 3/5 compares the shaded parts with the unshaded parts.', 'AC9M4N03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('a9452240-bb64-4007-b058-9a6ccc5dfb2e', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which fraction is the same as the shaded part of the strip?', '["2/3","3/4","4/10","1/4"]'::jsonb, 0, '4 of the 6 equal parts are shaded, which is 4/6. Halving the top and the bottom gives 2/3.', 'AC9M4N03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('7cfd61da-296d-4f14-bbc3-02afb2cb5d31', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'A school bus can carry 48 students.
How many students can 3 buses carry?', null, null, '3 × 48 = 144. One way: 3 × 40 = 120 and 3 × 8 = 24, and 120 + 24 = 144.', 'AC9M4N07', null, null, true, '144 students', ARRAY['144']::text[], null, null),
('1555fea4-a76d-4f75-9039-08396ed17cd1', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What number is the arrow pointing to?', '["0.07","1.7","7","0.7"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The number line from 0 to 1 is split into 10 equal parts, so each part is one tenth, 0.1. The arrow is at 7 tenths, which is 0.7.', 'AC9M4N04', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('b1015fda-45cc-425c-b3ab-96b3e14cd63c', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these numbers is the largest?', '["0.6","0.45","0.09","0.58"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Compare the tenths first: 0.6 has 6 tenths, 0.58 has 5, 0.45 has 4 and 0.09 has none. So 0.6 is the largest, even though 0.58 has more digits.', 'AC9M4N04', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('cbf7e33a-1008-49f7-b3b0-211098c7f490', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Maya buys a sandwich and two drinks from the canteen.
How much does she spend?', null, null, 'Two drinks cost $2.20 + $2.20 = $4.40. With the sandwich: $4.50 + $4.40 = $8.90.', 'AC9M4N08', null, null, true, '$8.90', ARRAY['8.90', '8.9']::text[], null, null),
('e78de92b-c868-4704-a26c-c84c2dee88b9', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What is 3486 rounded to the nearest hundred?', '["3400","3490","3500","4000"]'::jsonb, 2, '3486 is between 3400 and 3500. The tens digit is 8, so it is closer to 3500. 3490 is rounded to the nearest ten, not hundred.', 'AC9M4N02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('c36b1dba-67a9-4978-8b94-7915fe4faa0d', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which group of coins makes exactly $1.35?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Group D is $1 + 20c + 10c + 5c = $1.35. Group A makes $1.40, group B makes $1.25 and group C makes $1.30.', 'AC9M4N08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('3d378339-628e-45fa-8a1c-b2f2e9938eef', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'Tia has 125 marbles.
She gives 38 of them to her brother.
How many marbles does Tia have left?', null, null, '125 − 38 = 87. One way: 125 − 30 = 95, then 95 − 8 = 87.', 'AC9M4N05', null, null, true, '87 marbles', ARRAY['87']::text[], null, null),
('43420b0d-68a6-47fc-805b-a478eb0d0c52', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A spider has 8 legs.
Which number sentence could be used to find how many legs 7 spiders have?', '["7 + 8","8 − 7","8 × 7","56 ÷ 8"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each of the 7 spiders has 8 legs, so there are 7 groups of 8: 8 × 7. Adding 7 + 8 counts only one spider’s legs plus the number of spiders.', 'AC9M4N06', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('6ca6b930-e636-473a-a8bc-9289932b9528', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Sam adds two odd numbers together.
What kind of number is the answer?', '["always odd","sometimes odd and sometimes even","always a multiple of 4","always even"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An odd number is an even number plus 1, so two odd numbers add to an even number plus 2, which is even. For example, 3 + 5 = 8 and 7 + 9 = 16.', 'AC9M4N02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('d6dc9daa-5a24-4fa0-8bd1-a4a916043cfc', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'A movie ticket costs $15.
Harry has saved $9.
How much more money does he need?', '["$5","$6","$9","$24"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The difference between $15 and $9 is $6, because 9 + 6 = 15. $24 adds the amounts instead.', 'AC9M4N05', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('6681d936-9adb-47e2-8d82-39191a327d87', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'In which number does the digit 5 have a value of 500?', '["1052","2531","5210","3125"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In 2531 the 5 is in the hundreds place, so it is worth 500. In 1052 it is worth 50, in 5210 it is worth 5000 and in 3125 it is worth 5.', 'AC9M4N01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('aaeeb4e4-ac6c-48b3-a7f8-1317d48ab908', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Lucy reads 12 pages of her book every day.
How many pages does she read in 2 weeks?', null, null, '2 weeks is 14 days, and 14 × 12 = 168 pages.', 'AC9M4N07', null, null, true, '168 pages', ARRAY['168']::text[], null, null),
('90d57601-0155-4317-977f-6a6069584496', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '36 students are going on an excursion.
Each car can take 5 students.
What is the smallest number of cars they need?', '["6","7","8","31"]'::jsonb, 2, '7 cars can take 7 × 5 = 35 students, which leaves 1 student with no seat, so they need 8 cars. Answering 7 ignores the student left over.', 'AC9M4N07', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('97474f65-cd99-4bff-adb9-9b4a5e540d7c', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'There are 20 apples in a box.
One quarter of them are green.
How many apples are green?', '["4","5","15","80"]'::jsonb, 1, 'One quarter means 20 shared into 4 equal groups, which is 5 in each group. So 5 apples are green.', 'AC9M4N03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('5937f25e-2169-442b-b4f2-230093349e21', 'number_operations', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The scale is balanced.
What is the mass of the bag?', '["250 g","750 g","850 g","1250 g"]'::jsonb, 1, '1 kg is 1000 g. The bag and the 250 g mass together balance 1000 g, so the bag is 1000 − 250 = 750 g.', 'AC9M4M01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('074ad4c9-c68e-4028-a00b-b67157fa9e91', 'number_patterns', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Jo is making a pattern with counters.
If the pattern continues, how many counters will Step 5 have?', '["9","10","11","13"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The steps have 3, 5 and 7 counters, so 2 more counters are added each time. Step 4 has 9 and Step 5 has 11.', 'AC9M4A01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('e6fc11ba-f080-4cce-8ee7-7bf94ab8ed7c', 'number_patterns', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The numbers in this pattern go up by the same amount each time.
7, 13, 19, 25, …
What is the next number?', '["30","31","32","35"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each number is 6 more than the one before, so the next number is 25 + 6 = 31.', 'AC9M4A01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('af9d1efd-8d44-4798-9068-46b08baf3fda', 'number_patterns', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'What number goes in the box?
□ + 27 = 63', null, null, 'The missing number and 27 add to 63, so it is 63 − 27 = 36. Check: 36 + 27 = 63.', 'AC9M4A01', null, null, true, '36', null, null, null),
('9dde2d96-95db-443a-8c7d-f1e152d4a447', 'number_patterns', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows the cost of tickets to the school play.
If the pattern continues, how much will 6 tickets cost?', '["$20","$22","$24","$28"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each ticket costs $4, so 6 tickets cost 6 × 4 = $24.', 'AC9M4A02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('c0797608-36e4-4d08-a31c-db2e93ef5759', 'number_patterns', 'grade_4', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Ari counts backwards by 25s, starting at 300.
Which of these numbers will he say?', '["230","215","175","160"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Counting back by 25s from 300 gives 275, 250, 225, 200, 175, … so Ari will say 175. The other numbers are not multiples of 25.', 'AC9M4A02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('ad025a37-4170-43d7-8ace-4a7a5f727b80', 'number_patterns', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which number sentence is true?', '["42 ÷ 6 = 7","6 × 4 = 30","9 × 3 = 24","35 ÷ 5 = 6"]'::jsonb, 0, '42 ÷ 6 = 7 because 6 × 7 = 42. The others are wrong: 6 × 4 = 24, 9 × 3 = 27 and 35 ÷ 5 = 7.', 'AC9M4A02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('b4a302fc-237c-4b39-80e3-95b661950599', 'number_patterns', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Kim thinks of a number.
She doubles it and then adds 5.
Her answer is 29.
What number did she think of?', null, null, 'Work backwards: 29 − 5 = 24, and half of 24 is 12. Check: 12 doubled is 24, and 24 + 5 = 29.', 'AC9M4A01', null, null, true, '12', null, null, null),
('c55f2f9e-dccc-4852-ab82-8f94a2a07b40', 'number_patterns', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Each design is a square made of small tiles.
How many small tiles will be in Design 4?', '["12","13","20","16"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Design 4 will be a square 4 tiles wide and 4 tiles high, so it has 4 × 4 = 16 tiles. Adding the same number each time does not work here: the designs have 1, 4 and 9 tiles.', 'AC9M4A01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('91f214cf-4b5c-42a8-ab18-e4e16baed6d9', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'foundation', 'multiple_choice', 'What time does the clock show?', '["3:45","9:15","4:45","9:03"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The long minute hand points to the 9, which is 45 minutes past. The short hour hand is between 3 and 4, so it is 3:45. 4:45 reads the hour from the number the hand is nearest.', 'AC9M4M03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('3bfbec32-d429-4987-bbc1-3997db9d223a', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The clock shows the time Mia’s swimming lesson starts.
The lesson lasts 45 minutes.
What time does it finish?', '["4:15","5:15","5:45","6:15"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lesson starts at 4:30. 30 minutes later it is 5:00, and another 15 minutes makes 5:15.', 'AC9M4M03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('13162455-ed32-4644-9ae6-ac4901670fcf', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'How long is the pencil?', null, null, 'The pencil starts at 2 cm and ends at 9.5 cm, so it is 9.5 − 2 = 7.5 cm long. Reading only the end gives 9.5 cm, which is wrong because the pencil does not start at 0.', 'AC9M4M01', null, null, true, '7.5 cm', ARRAY['7.5', '7½ cm', '7 and a half cm']::text[], null, null),
('0d32909f-2352-462a-a06a-2ce81f2e8270', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'How much water is in the jug?', '["70 mL","600 mL","700 mL","800 mL"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The labelled marks go up by 200 mL, so the small mark between two labels is 100 mL. The water is one small mark above 600, at 700 mL.', 'AC9M4M01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('7ef97aff-1d62-40c8-82fa-8fb704704393', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'What is the area of the shaded shape, in square centimetres?', null, null, 'Count the shaded squares: 4 + 4 + 2 + 2 = 12. Each square is 1 square centimetre, so the area is 12 square centimetres.', 'AC9M4M02', null, null, true, '12', ARRAY['12 cm²', '12 square centimetres', '12 square cm']::text[], null, null),
('7878564b-2373-4af4-9a40-ef52882734f6', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the perimeter of the shaded shape?', '["4 cm","8 cm","10 cm","12 cm"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Go around the outside edge and count the sides of small squares: there are 10, so the perimeter is 10 cm. 4 is the number of squares, which is the area.', 'AC9M4M02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('1cbe11b6-f189-444e-baea-94ff9f02d5ce', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which angle is larger than a right angle?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 2, 'A right angle is a square corner, like angle B. Angle C opens wider than that, so it is larger than a right angle. Angles A and D are smaller.', 'AC9M4M04', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('179cf06c-f095-43b2-8615-be8f386620cd', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Sam starts at the library.
He walks 3 squares east and then 2 squares north.
Where does he finish?', '["at the pool","at the park","at the shop","at the school"]'::jsonb, 0, '3 squares east from B2 is E2, and then 2 squares north is E4, where the pool is. Stopping after going east only would leave him at the park.', 'AC9M4SP02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('d739d6d2-2ecc-40e4-b607-f6072d42d74e', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which shape has its line of symmetry drawn correctly?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 1, 'If you fold shape B along its dashed line, the two halves match exactly. The lines through shapes A, C and D do not split them into matching halves.', 'AC9M4SP03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('71f5754a-4fde-4c8f-be44-6880d29b98d5', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these is a triangular prism?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 3, 'A triangular prism has two matching triangle faces joined by rectangles, like object D. Object A is a cube, B is a square pyramid and C is a cylinder.', 'AC9M4SP01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('c89ede8e-e811-4a87-bd94-a1f0e1645991', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The calendar shows the month of March.
The school camp starts on the second Tuesday in March and lasts for 3 days.
On what date in March does the camp finish?', null, null, 'The Tuesdays in March are the 7th, 14th, 21st and 28th, so the second Tuesday is the 14th. The camp runs on the 14th, 15th and 16th, so it finishes on the 16th.', 'AC9M4M03', null, null, true, '16', ARRAY['16 March', '16th', '16th March', 'the 16th']::text[], null, null),
('06d69e2b-f885-46d0-920f-38998cc1ede5', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the mass of the watermelon on the scale?', '["3.5 kg","3 kg","4 kg","35 kg"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The needle is halfway between 3 and 4, and each small mark is half a kilogram, so the watermelon is 3.5 kg.', 'AC9M4M01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('a7f18d1f-9906-4b48-8259-036f1f74dc0f', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'How many minutes are there in two and a half hours?', '["120","130","150","250"]'::jsonb, 2, 'One hour is 60 minutes, so two hours is 120 minutes and half an hour is 30 more. 120 + 30 = 150 minutes.', 'AC9M4M03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('6e1ef78a-b881-4b9e-859c-b0ce30235410', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The model is made of cubes, with no gaps.
How many cubes are used to make the model?', '["4","5","6","7"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The back row has a stack of 2 and a single cube, and the front row has 2 single cubes. 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5 cubes.', 'AC9M4SP01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('572a24a5-de88-4b5e-ad3d-3908f0557370', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'This rectangular vegetable garden needs a fence all the way around it.
How many metres of fencing are needed?', null, null, 'The fence goes along all four sides: 8 + 5 + 8 + 5 = 26 m. Only adding the two sides shown gives 13 m.', 'AC9M4M02', null, null, true, '26 m', ARRAY['26']::text[], null, null),
('c61cec25-f9bd-4857-b5af-0776c093621c', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What temperature does the thermometer show?', '["21 °C","25 °C","32 °C","23 °C"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The top of the liquid is 3 small marks above 20, and each mark is 1 degree, so the temperature is 23 °C.', 'AC9M4M01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('aa88b73a-0497-47ba-93f5-22bbc872ea1c', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which object has 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 corners?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 3, 'A cube has 6 square faces, 12 edges and 8 corners. The square pyramid has 5 faces, the triangular prism has 5 faces and the cylinder has no corners.', 'AC9M4SP01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('75fa4850-6119-4db6-b591-cee2030c19b4', 'geometry_measurement', 'grade_4', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'How many right angles are there inside this shape?', '["2","3","4","5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two bottom corners are right angles, and so is the corner at the top point, where the two sloping sides meet like the corner of a square. The two corners where the sides slope in are larger than a right angle. That makes 3.', 'AC9M4M04', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('729e66ff-3d0a-4c17-b651-f0ec7667c7c5', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The pictograph shows how many books four children read over the holidays.
How many books did Ben read?', '["3.5","6","7","8"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each whole book picture stands for 2 books and half a picture stands for 1 book. Ben has 3 whole pictures and a half: 6 + 1 = 7 books.', 'AC9M4ST02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('40681c24-fff5-4fc4-982a-67ea4c406995', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The column graph shows the favourite fruit of the students in Class 4R.
How many students chose apples or bananas?', null, null, '8 students chose apples and 6 chose bananas, and 8 + 6 = 14.', 'AC9M4ST02', null, null, true, '14', null, null, null),
('33d89936-181a-4d13-91e5-0e99d33a761c', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The tally shows how the students in Class 4B travel to school.
How many students ride a bike?', '["4","7","9","12"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The bike row has one group of five tally marks and 2 more, which is 7.', 'AC9M4ST02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('1a121618-7a35-4e83-8fd1-13453eb44a28', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which colour is this spinner most likely to land on?', '["red","blue","yellow","all colours are equally likely"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Red covers 3 of the 6 equal parts, more than any other colour, so red is the most likely.', 'AC9M4P01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('2fca08f4-2049-46b4-b7d6-0f0c708b7b37', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these events is impossible?', '["Tossing a coin and getting heads","Picking a red card from a full deck of playing cards","It rains somewhere in Australia this year","Rolling a 7 on a standard six-sided dice"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A standard dice has the numbers 1 to 6, so rolling a 7 cannot happen. The other events can happen.', 'AC9M4P01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('cd05f8fb-0581-4d7f-a04a-a21da356b103', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows the pets owned by students in a class.
Which pictograph shows the same information?', '["","","",""]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each face stands for 2 pets. 6 dogs is 3 faces, 4 cats is 2 faces, and 3 fish is 1 and a half faces. Only pictograph C shows this.', 'AC9M4ST02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('26cc0491-437e-42a2-ac84-88df2e455868', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'advanced', 'short_answer', 'The graph shows how many goals each team scored this season.
How many more goals did the Hawks score than the Owls?', null, null, 'The Hawks scored 18 goals. The Owls’ bar ends halfway between 10 and 12, which is 11. 18 − 11 = 7.', 'AC9M4ST02', null, null, true, '7', null, null, null),
('962688c1-04b9-4219-8c4c-cec708081081', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows how many cans each class collected for recycling.
Which class collected the most cans?', '["4A","4B","4C","4D"]'::jsonb, 1, '182 is the largest number in the table, so class 4B collected the most. 181 is close, but it is one less.', 'AC9M4ST03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('3c40ef59-8cf3-4941-b671-20b9085ec6d8', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'A bag has 5 red marbles and 1 blue marble.
Nina takes out one marble without looking.
Which word best describes the chance that it is red?', '["impossible","unlikely","likely","certain"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Most of the marbles are red, so red is likely. It is not certain, because she could pick the blue one.', 'AC9M4P01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('897f6b13-356f-4d6f-b5f6-070a28cf66f6', 'statistics_probability', 'grade_4', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'The dot plot shows how many pets each student in a class has.
How many students have 2 pets?', null, null, 'Each dot is one student. There are 5 dots above the 2, so 5 students have 2 pets.', 'AC9M4ST02', null, null, true, '5', ARRAY['5 students']::text[], null, null),
('ff9c8c57-1f7a-4caf-b7dd-41186254a7c3', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Point A shows the temperature at dawn and point B shows the temperature at noon.
By how many degrees did the temperature rise?', '["2","4","10","−10"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dawn was −6 °C and noon was 4 °C. From −6 up to 0 is 6 degrees, and from 0 up to 4 is 4 more, so the rise is 10 degrees. Answering 2 just finds the difference between 6 and 4.', 'AC9M6N01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('7a59b4b5-eba5-4f65-a867-36dd9b0a2cd3', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these numbers is a prime number?', '["29","21","27","33"]'::jsonb, 0, '29 has only two factors, 1 and 29, so it is prime. 21 = 3 × 7, 27 = 3 × 9 and 33 = 3 × 11 all have other factors.', 'AC9M6N02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('340aeaee-45ff-497e-99b2-40b1ef854cdb', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'A packet of 24 pencils costs $6.
How much does one pencil cost?', null, null, '$6 is 600 cents, and 600 ÷ 24 = 25, so each pencil costs 25 cents, which is $0.25.', 'AC9M6N07', null, null, true, '$0.25', ARRAY['0.25', '25c', '25 cents', '.25']::text[], null, null),
('6c48a545-73c7-413f-b51d-027cb63e2e2e', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The bar model shows 36 split into four equal parts.
What is 3/4 of 36?', '["9","12","27","48"]'::jsonb, 2, 'One quarter of 36 is 36 ÷ 4 = 9, so three quarters is 3 × 9 = 27. The answer 9 is only one quarter.', 'AC9M6N06', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('cc108a9b-0bbe-44c8-af9e-35bb58b8066f', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these is equal to 40%?', '["0.04","2/5","4","1/40"]'::jsonb, 1, '40% means 40 out of 100, which is 40/100. Dividing the top and bottom by 20 gives 2/5. 40% as a decimal is 0.4, not 0.04.', 'AC9M6N05', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('7dccbfc3-8f04-4c28-93ab-d55176237b34', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Everything in the shop is 25% off.
What is the sale price of the backpack?', '["$12","$23","$36","$44"]'::jsonb, 2, '25% is one quarter, and a quarter of $48 is $12. The sale price is $48 − $12 = $36. Taking $25 off gives $23, which treats 25% as $25.', 'AC9M6N09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('a7c2e2cb-bcde-45d7-bd5d-70f45df3e7fa', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Evie buys 3 notebooks that cost $2.45 each.
She pays with a $10 note.
How much change does she get?', null, null, 'The notebooks cost 3 × $2.45 = $7.35. The change is $10.00 − $7.35 = $2.65.', 'AC9M6N09', null, null, true, '$2.65', ARRAY['2.65']::text[], null, null),
('f0b115e7-f064-4fbe-8dae-1e7c6ca236e2', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the value of 18 − 3 × (2 + 4)?', '["0","12","90","96"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Work out the brackets first: 2 + 4 = 6. Then multiply: 3 × 6 = 18. Last, subtract: 18 − 18 = 0. Working left to right gives 15 × 6 = 90, which is wrong.', 'AC9M6N08', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('863782cb-dcfe-4037-9f2c-e5d121565910', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which number is shown by the arrow?', '["2.07","2.75","3.7","2.7"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The space from 2 to 3 is split into 10 equal parts, so each mark is 0.1. The arrow is 7 marks past 2, at 2.7.', 'AC9M6N03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('9b658c4e-fd87-462e-94dc-a4565f857af4', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'A recipe needs 2½ cups of flour for one cake.
How many cups of flour are needed for 3 cakes?', null, null, '3 lots of 2 cups is 6 cups, and 3 lots of half a cup is 1½ cups. Altogether that is 7½ cups.', 'AC9M6N04', null, null, true, '7½', ARRAY['7.5', '7 1/2', '7 and a half', '7½ cups', '7.5 cups']::text[], null, null),
('82031270-bb51-4e54-905e-1686f06511d4', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these numbers is the smallest?', '["0.305","0.35","0.053","0.5"]'::jsonb, 2, '0.053 has no tenths, while all the others have at least 3 tenths, so 0.053 is the smallest. Having more digits does not make a decimal bigger.', 'AC9M6N03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('3022366a-2d13-4f4d-b074-b8171eb6af23', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'What fraction of the rectangle is shaded, in its simplest form?', '["2/3","3/4","4/12","8/4"]'::jsonb, 0, '8 of the 12 equal squares are shaded, which is 8/12. Dividing the top and bottom by 4 gives 2/3. 4/12 is the unshaded part.', 'AC9M6N04', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('ca8d7f8a-7057-4b9e-8515-3e1dfcb0c4e2', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What is 4.8 × 100?', '["0.048","48","4800","480"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Multiplying by 100 makes each digit worth 100 times as much, which moves the digits two places to the left: 4.8 × 100 = 480.', 'AC9M6N03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('6aecbd8a-b31f-47c3-b07f-2528d8a99f9a', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Tim had $50 and bought everything on this receipt.
How much money does he have left?', '["$19.70","$29.30","$31.30","$30.30"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The receipt adds to $9.80 + $7.50 + $2.40 = $19.70. Tim has $50.00 − $19.70 = $30.30 left.', 'AC9M6N09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('e1df3bb4-190a-432e-8f5f-ee544c693c8b', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'What is the next square number after 49?', null, null, '49 = 7 × 7, so the next square number is 8 × 8 = 64.', 'AC9M6N02', null, null, true, '64', null, null, null),
('03195597-5aa1-4db6-a2e1-bcdca430edcf', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Nadia’s family drove 450 km on the first day of their holiday.
On the second day they drove 3/5 of that distance.
How far did they drive on the second day?', null, null, 'One fifth of 450 km is 90 km, so three fifths is 3 × 90 = 270 km.', 'AC9M6N06', null, null, true, '270 km', ARRAY['270']::text[], null, null),
('c12e6568-2d78-40fe-9fa7-a02da3a59a20', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Which statement is always true?', '["A prime number is odd.","An even number multiplied by any whole number is even.","A square number is even.","An odd number plus an odd number is odd."]'::jsonb, 1, 'An even number has a factor of 2, so any whole number times it still has a factor of 2 and is even. The others fail: 2 is prime and even, 9 is square and odd, and 3 + 5 = 8 is even.', 'AC9M6N02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('1baec244-defc-46fd-b6ba-74e15dade57b', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'The price list shows four sizes of bottled water.
Which size is the best value?', '["600 mL","1 L","1.5 L","2 L"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Compare the price of one litre: 600 mL works out at $3.00 a litre, 1 L is $2.50, 1.5 L is $2.20 and 2 L is $2.30. The 1.5 L bottle is cheapest per litre.', 'AC9M6N09', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('9303ec64-2a28-429d-82de-0e97b0765447', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'Which fraction is equal to 0.75?', '["3/4","7/5","75/10","1/75"]'::jsonb, 0, '0.75 is 75 hundredths, which is 75/100. Dividing the top and bottom by 25 gives 3/4.', 'AC9M6N05', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('fc847a76-06e5-4b5f-9436-fce89e500394', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Ella had $23 in her bank account.
She paid a bill of $40 from the account.
What is her balance now?', null, null, 'She paid $17 more than she had: 23 − 40 = −17. Her account is overdrawn, and the balance is −$17.', 'AC9M6N01', null, null, true, '−$17', ARRAY['-17', '-$17', '−17', '$-17', 'minus 17', '-17 dollars']::text[], null, null),
('b930ae23-ecc6-4355-a001-1507a8bd2730', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'There are 30 students in a class.
40% of them walk to school.
How many students do not walk to school?', '["12","18","20","26"]'::jsonb, 1, '10% of 30 is 3, so 40% is 12 students who walk. The rest, 30 − 12 = 18, do not walk. 12 is the number who do walk.', 'AC9M6N06', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('3090233e-20cd-4d76-a11f-a574b7c7dbf8', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'What is 7.48 rounded to one decimal place?', '["7.4","7.5","7.48","8"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The hundredths digit is 8, which is 5 or more, so the tenths digit goes up from 4 to 5: 7.5.', 'AC9M6N03', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('4d0d845b-191e-4d7e-a6e5-48f26063de7d', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Chloe ate 1/4 of a pizza and Max ate 3/8 of the same pizza.
What fraction of the pizza did they eat altogether?', '["4/12","4/8","3/4","5/8"]'::jsonb, 3, '1/4 is the same as 2/8, and 2/8 + 3/8 = 5/8. Adding the tops and the bottoms to get 4/12 is a common mistake.', 'AC9M6N04', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('e3173a81-d3b6-4f7b-a8a7-33186ab95b8b', 'number_operations', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'What number is 1000 more than 49 520?', null, null, 'Adding 1000 adds 1 to the thousands digit. 49 thousands plus 1 thousand is 50 thousands, so the answer is 50 520.', 'AC9M6N01', null, null, true, '50 520', ARRAY['50520', '50,520']::text[], null, null),
('e13f12e4-b2b8-4e01-a37f-20e2e2310c05', 'number_patterns', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'Aiden makes this pattern with tiles.
If the pattern continues, how many tiles will be in Design 6?', '["16","18","19","21"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The designs have 4, 7 and 10 tiles, so 3 tiles are added each time. Design 4 has 13, Design 5 has 16 and Design 6 has 19.', 'AC9M6A01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('a4e647e4-dc73-46f1-b16e-ca65b3a2d567', 'number_patterns', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The table shows the numbers going into and coming out of a number machine.
What rule does the machine use?', '["multiply by 3, then add 1","add 5","multiply by 2, then add 3","multiply by 4, then subtract 1"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Check each rule against every row. Multiplying by 3 and adding 1 works for all of them: 2 → 7, 4 → 13, 5 → 16 and 8 → 25. The other rules work for the first row only.', 'AC9M6A01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('0143cc5c-0b5e-49d4-9bc0-0af6c530b855', 'number_patterns', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'short_answer', 'What number goes in the box?
7 × □ = 84', null, null, '84 ÷ 7 = 12. Check: 7 × 12 = 84.', 'AC9M6A02', null, null, true, '12', null, null, null),
('92cbe399-eb29-4123-95ec-95746c3316a0', 'number_patterns', 'grade_6', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A number pattern starts at 3.
Each number after that is double the previous number, plus 1.
What is the fifth number in the pattern?', '["31","48","63","127"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pattern goes 3, 7, 15, 31, 63. For example, the second number is 3 doubled plus 1, which is 7. The fifth number is 63; 31 is the fourth.', 'AC9M6A01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('678ed8f3-781b-404e-b8ee-ad855d7994d1', 'number_patterns', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'multiple_choice', 'The points on the grid follow a pattern.
If the pattern continues, what are the coordinates of the next point?', '["(4, 7)","(4, 8)","(5, 8)","(8, 4)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each point is 1 to the right and 2 up from the one before, so the next point is (4, 8). The answer (8, 4) swaps the x and y values.', 'AC9M6SP02', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('3dcfb150-0741-431d-865f-4f1551504e35', 'number_patterns', 'grade_6', 'developing', 'multiple_choice', 'The first number in a pattern is 2.4.
Each number after that is 0.3 less than the one before it.
What is the fourth number in the pattern?', '["1.2","1.8","3.3","1.5"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pattern goes 2.4, 2.1, 1.8, 1.5, so the fourth number is 1.5. The answer 1.8 is the third number.', 'AC9M6A01', null, null, true, null, null, null, null),
('f84290b1-0b8e-49de-95a8-57bef3034d4f', 'number_patterns', 'grade_6', 'proficient', 'short_answer', 'Liam saves $6 in the first week.
